WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.900
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4641.

2
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:01.800
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:08.700
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Alright today is Wednesday July thirteenth, 2016 and I’m here with Mr. Hal McMillin at the Imperial Calcasieu Museum in Lake Charles [Louisiana].

4
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:13.200
And Mr. Hal I have your list of accomplishments right here and they’re quite lengthy.

5
00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.800
You were the Westlake city councilman from 1995 through 1999, police juror,

6
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.600
vice president of the police juror, president of the police juror, and the list goes on.

7
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.500
So we’ll definitely get more into like, your work and your work history,

8
00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:30.000
but first I have everyone just state their name, full name for the record.

9
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.000
<v HAL MCMILLIN>Yeah and that’s a good thing to do.

10
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:38.625
Usually whenever I do an interview I always state my name and spell it, also.

11
00:00:38.625 --> 00:00:39.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

12
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.500
<v MCMILLIN>Because it’s Hal, H-A-L.

13
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:42.375
And then McMillin is M-C-M-I-L-L-I-N.

14
00:00:42.375 --> 00:00:51.000
I always say like penicillin, ends in I-N because a lot of people try to put an O-N or O-N stuff like this.

15
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.500
But family name’s McMillin.

16
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.375
Very proud of that name.

17
00:00:54.375 --> 00:01:00.000
My parents originated from up in North Louisiana and moved to this area in 1955,

18
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:06.375
so I’m excited about my history and being a part of this area basically my entire life.

19
00:01:06.375 --> 00:01:10.875
I’ve, you know, I was born here in Lake Charles and I’m

20
00:01:10.875 --> 00:01:12.000
<c.p>sixty years old.

21
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.500
Be sixty-one in November.

22
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What’s your birthday?

23
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:16.076
<v MCMILLIN>November eighteenth, 1955.

24
00:01:16.076 --> 00:01:19.662
And it’s been a great place to grow up in.

25
00:01:19.662 --> 00:01:24.683
God has blessed me that he let me grow up here in southwest Louisiana.

26
00:01:24.683 --> 00:01:26.834
A place that I truly love.

27
00:01:26.834 --> 00:01:31.855
I mean if you . . . A young man of my interest and character coming up.

28
00:01:31.855 --> 00:01:39.028
The hunting and fishing, all the things with the outdoors and growing up in a family that really enjoyed that.

29
00:01:39.028 --> 00:01:42.614
It was tremendous to be right here in southwest Louisiana.

30
00:01:42.614 --> 00:01:49.428
You know, we have saltwater fishing, and freshwater fishing, ducks, geese, doves, rabbits, everything that you could possibly want.

31
00:01:49.428 --> 00:01:56.959
And I always say the best food and of course the prettiest women in the world right here in southwest Louisiana.

32
00:01:56.959 --> 00:02:01.621
So it’s a . . . it’s quite an honor to be here; grow up here.

33
00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:02.697
It’s my home.

34
00:02:02.697 --> 00:02:03.772
I love it.

35
00:02:03.772 --> 00:02:07.000
I wouldn’t want to live anywhere else, so.

36
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.976
<v ARSENEAULT>What brought your parents from . . . Where in North Louisiana?

37
00:02:09.976 --> 00:02:13.612
<v MCMILLIN>My dad was a . . . Yeah my dad was from Jonesville, Louisiana.

38
00:02:13.612 --> 00:02:15.927
And he was a hometown plumbing contractor.

39
00:02:15.927 --> 00:02:19.233
And my mom, the way I got interested in politics,

40
00:02:19.233 --> 00:02:27.829
she was the city clerk for the city of Westlake back in the 70s and I used to always go . . . from the 60s to the 70s.

41
00:02:27.829 --> 00:02:29.482
Late 60s to the 70s.

42
00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:38.739
My dad wasn’t a guy that would like to put on a coat and tie and go to conventions so I would always follow my mom to conventions,

43
00:02:38.739 --> 00:02:41.714
the Louisiana Municipal Association back when I was young.

44
00:02:41.714 --> 00:02:44.359
So I’ve always had an interest in politics.

45
00:02:44.359 --> 00:02:50.971
And I guess being that November birthday, a Scorpio male, I’ve always been a leader and I’ve always led most

46
00:02:50.971 --> 00:02:52.294
<c.p>everything that I’ve done.

47
00:02:52.294 --> 00:03:00.229
So that was some of the history of my parents moving here, us growing up, Westlake, the hometown that I grew up in tremendous.

48
00:03:00.229 --> 00:03:08.163
We were probably three to four thousand people back in the days of growing up and it was the greatest place in the world.

49
00:03:08.163 --> 00:03:17.090
I mean you could . . . The summer times were filled with bicycles with a baseball glove on it heading up to the recreation center and the swimming pool.

50
00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:18.412
An easy, easy life.

51
00:03:18.412 --> 00:03:25.024
My dad was as I said a hometown plumbing contractor, and my mom was city clerk for city of Westlake.

52
00:03:25.024 --> 00:03:28.000
And she worked at banks and stuff like . . .

53
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:29.400
<v ARSENEAULT>What were their names?

54
00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.150
<v MCMILLIN>Harold McMillin and Dalton McMillin.

55
00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:33.250
My mom had a guy’s name.

56
00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:33.950
Dalton Edith.

57
00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:35.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Her maiden?

58
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:36.431
<v MCMILLIN>She was a [Welch?].

59
00:03:36.431 --> 00:03:38.219
Her parents were from Oakdale.

60
00:03:38.219 --> 00:03:43.226
Louisiana roots all the way, but they moved here like I say from Jonesville.

61
00:03:43.226 --> 00:03:49.307
We grew up in a family of . . . I had an older brother seventeen years older than me.

62
00:03:49.307 --> 00:03:55.387
I had another big brother ten years older than me, a sister eight years older than me.

63
00:03:55.387 --> 00:03:57.175
<v ARSENEAULT>So you were the baby?

64
00:03:57.175 --> 00:03:58.964
<v MCMILLIN>I was a baby, definitely.

65
00:03:58.964 --> 00:03:59.679
Spoiled rotten.

66
00:03:59.679 --> 00:04:03.613
I had . . . My nearest brother graduated from high school in ’63,

67
00:04:03.613 --> 00:04:09.336
and I graduated in 1973 from Westlake High and then later went to McNeese [State University],

68
00:04:09.336 --> 00:04:17.204
but the interest of your older brother from the 60s and the little baby brother who was spoiled rotten from the 70s,

69
00:04:17.204 --> 00:04:24.000
we were . . . We’re pretty different, but we’ve grown to really love each other and enjoy each other’s company.

70
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:28.759
One of the common bonds we had is we’re both pretty big duck hunters.

71
00:04:28.759 --> 00:04:33.858
I would say that the McMillin family, my son, his son, the family we have,

72
00:04:33.858 --> 00:04:39.976
when it comes to duck hunting we would rank right up there with the Robertson’s of North Louisiana.

73
00:04:39.976 --> 00:04:42.016
We’re just as serious about it.

74
00:04:42.016 --> 00:04:45.415
And started guiding in early 70s at the Chateaux Charles.

75
00:04:45.415 --> 00:04:51.534
A lot of things that tied me to this community, tied me both to Westlake and to Mossville.

76
00:04:51.534 --> 00:04:52.893
It’s been fun life.

77
00:04:52.893 --> 00:05:00.711
I’ve had a great time and I’ve always tried to I guess God given gift is I’m a communicator and a people person.

78
00:05:00.711 --> 00:05:12.269
So that’s where the things that I do and things that I enjoy in life have kind of combined all that together to put me where we’re at with this whole police jury stuff.

79
00:05:12.269 --> 00:05:14.988
And having . . . Going to Westlake High and S.P.

80
00:05:14.988 --> 00:05:17.028
Arnett and elementary school there, too.

81
00:05:17.028 --> 00:05:25.866
A number of the kids that we grew up with all the way through life are still in the community and we see them every day.

82
00:05:25.866 --> 00:05:32.664
Whether it’s at the ballfield, whether our kids or grandkids now, or the grocery store, you run across those people.

83
00:05:32.664 --> 00:05:34.704
Westlake’s a very very good hometown.

84
00:05:34.704 --> 00:05:41.162
And Moss Bluff, although not incorporated in Westlake, had a lot of the same common things that Westlake had.

85
00:05:41.162 --> 00:05:45.581
Big families that knew each other and we all went to school together.

86
00:05:45.581 --> 00:05:50.000
So, but those are some of the questions I’ll let you ask.

87
00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:50.486
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, sure.

88
00:05:50.486 --> 00:05:51.946
<v MCMILLIN>Where do we need to go?

89
00:05:51.946 --> 00:05:52.919
<v ARSENEAULT>Those are all great.

90
00:05:52.919 --> 00:05:57.541
That’s great information, but your . . . just further going back, kind of your origins and how your family got here.

91
00:05:57.541 --> 00:05:59.000
What were your grandparents’ names?

92
00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:06.295
<v MCMILLIN>Oh, my grandmother . . . I never knew my grandfather on my dad’s side, but my grandmother, her name is [Cattie?] McMillin.

93
00:06:06.295 --> 00:06:09.213
And my dad had a bunch of brothers.

94
00:06:09.213 --> 00:06:11.766
He had five brothers and three sisters.

95
00:06:11.766 --> 00:06:20.520
So all the first cousins in that part, when we would go up to Jonesville we were all close and we still stayed close.

96
00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:22.708
We still have a family reunion.

97
00:06:22.708 --> 00:06:24.897
So that’s on my dad’s side.

98
00:06:24.897 --> 00:06:27.085
My mom was an only child.

99
00:06:27.085 --> 00:06:31.097
So we didn’t have any . . . but my grandmother on that side,

100
00:06:31.097 --> 00:06:39.486
Bessie Welch and Charlie Welch who lived in Oakdale and remember trips of my childhood going up there and having a big gardens,

101
00:06:39.486 --> 00:06:43.863
and fig trees, and picking . . . digging up potatoes out of the ground.

102
00:06:43.863 --> 00:06:45.687
Just really good childhood memories.

103
00:06:45.687 --> 00:06:49.334
And then road trips going . . . leaving . . . when you were five,

104
00:06:49.334 --> 00:06:56.994
six years old to drive to Jonesville which was probably about two and half, three hours away, was a big trip.

105
00:06:56.994 --> 00:07:04.653
Or go to Oakdale which was an hour away was a . . . he’d load up the car and head out that way.

106
00:07:04.653 --> 00:07:12.313
That was in the days of young kids and no seatbelts and heading down the highway pand big adventures throughout there.

107
00:07:12.313 --> 00:07:16.690
So we . . . a really good core group of happiness in a childhood.

108
00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:19.243
I had . . . I never wanted for anything.

109
00:07:19.243 --> 00:07:24.714
Although we weren’t wealthy my dad was a very hard worker as a plumbing contractor.

110
00:07:24.714 --> 00:07:33.468
And that’s one of the things too I guess with the people of Mossville primarily being black people and Westlake having blacks and whites.

111
00:07:33.468 --> 00:07:37.116
My dad had a guy that worked with him named

112
00:07:37.116 --> 00:07:37.845
<c.p>Ernest Bates.

113
00:07:37.845 --> 00:07:42.222
And the Bates family was a very good black family from Westlake.

114
00:07:42.222 --> 00:07:44.775
Ernest was like a brother to me.

115
00:07:44.775 --> 00:07:48.787
So and my mom, with her working, and my dad working,

116
00:07:48.787 --> 00:07:54.623
we had a lady that would come in and take care of me during the day.

117
00:07:54.623 --> 00:07:55.717
A black lady.

118
00:07:55.717 --> 00:07:56.812
Whether a domestic . . .

119
00:07:56.812 --> 00:07:59.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember her name?

120
00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:01.933
<v MCMILLIN>Oh, there was three or four of them.

121
00:08:01.933 --> 00:08:02.300
Yeah.

122
00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:04.500
The first one was named Hazel.

123
00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:07.067
And Hazel had a daughter named Katie.

124
00:08:07.067 --> 00:08:09.633
And Katie was my first girlfriend ever.

125
00:08:09.633 --> 00:08:15.500
So my first girlfriend ever happened to be a little black girl back in the 60s,

126
00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:19.900
but that’s okay because that’s the way it should be in America.

127
00:08:19.900 --> 00:08:25.033
And I hope that America heals; that we can all be one as people.

128
00:08:25.033 --> 00:08:33.467
But, yeah I had Ms. Thelma, Ms. Hazel, Elvina, I got to say each one of them brought something different to the table.

129
00:08:33.467 --> 00:08:38.233
Ms. Hazel was a very good disciplinarian and she had Katie with her.

130
00:08:38.233 --> 00:08:44.100
Always liked when Katie would come we had a good time playing together, stuff like this.

131
00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:47.400
Ms. Perkins, Ms. Thelma Perkins, was probably my favorite.

132
00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:49.967
That’s why I love soul food today.

133
00:08:49.967 --> 00:08:52.900
She was one of the best cooks ever.

134
00:08:52.900 --> 00:08:54.000
I loved . . .

135
00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:55.629
<v ARSENEAULT>So she would cook when she . . .?

136
00:08:55.629 --> 00:08:59.974
<v MCMILLIN>Oh, she would . . . Yeah would cook lunch and Mom and Dad both come home for lunch.

137
00:08:59.974 --> 00:09:05.947
And then we would go . . . As kids we’d go to the recreation center, which I didn’t live a block or two from.

138
00:09:05.947 --> 00:09:07.305
They had a swimming pool.

139
00:09:07.305 --> 00:09:11.377
We’d go there . . . It opened at nine o’clock in the morning, stay there until noon.

140
00:09:11.377 --> 00:09:14.907
It closed from noon to one, then we’d go back at one o’clock.

141
00:09:14.907 --> 00:09:18.166
And that was an intermixing of black and white kids playing together.

142
00:09:18.166 --> 00:09:18.980
Back in the

143
00:09:18.980 --> 00:09:24.954
<c.p>60s and 70s we all played basketball together, we all played ping pong together, we didn’t have any prejudices in that era.

144
00:09:24.954 --> 00:09:29.026
So it was fun to have a lot of friends and grow up with that.

145
00:09:29.026 --> 00:09:32.828
But we’d always come home for lunch and that’s where I got spoiled to . . .

146
00:09:32.828 --> 00:09:35.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of things did she cook?

147
00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:38.438
<v MCMILLIN>Smothered pork chop, smothered chicken, beef tongue, tripe, fried chicken . . .

148
00:09:38.438 --> 00:09:40.844
<v ARSENEAULT>Now what is tripe, are those of . . .?

149
00:09:40.844 --> 00:09:42.562
<v MCMILLIN>Tripe is a cow stomach.

150
00:09:42.562 --> 00:09:43.594
And you use . . .

151
00:09:43.594 --> 00:09:46.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And how would she cook it?

152
00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:47.788
<v MCMILLIN>She would boil it . . . and salt and pepper.

153
00:09:47.788 --> 00:09:52.259
In fact, I still love it to this day, but my wife won’t let me cook it in my house.

154
00:09:52.259 --> 00:09:53.153
<v ARSENEAULT>Why not I wonder?

155
00:09:53.153 --> 00:09:54.494
<v MCMILLIN>It stinks when you cook it.

156
00:09:54.494 --> 00:09:54.941
<v ARSENEAULT>It smells?

157
00:09:54.941 --> 00:09:56.059
<v MCMILLIN>[Laughs] It’s got an aroma.

158
00:09:56.059 --> 00:09:58.071
It’s got a . . . I like the way it smells.

159
00:09:58.071 --> 00:09:59.412
My wife’s like, “Get that stinky . . .”

160
00:09:59.412 --> 00:10:00.082
<v ARSENEAULT>It’s like cabbage.

161
00:10:00.082 --> 00:10:02.094
Some people love it and some people hate it.

162
00:10:02.094 --> 00:10:03.659
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah, yeah some people love it, tripe.

163
00:10:03.659 --> 00:10:05.000
Tripe has an . . . It’s odiferous.

164
00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:05.756
<v ARSENEAULT>Distinctive odor.

165
00:10:05.756 --> 00:10:10.667
<v MCMILLIN>But yeah so I guess that’s some of the basics of growing up.

166
00:10:10.667 --> 00:10:12.556
I always loved soul food.

167
00:10:12.556 --> 00:10:17.089
I mean if I see a restaurant where there’s serving smothered anything,

168
00:10:17.089 --> 00:10:21.622
smothered pork chop, smothered chicken, rice . . . red beans and rice and gravy.

169
00:10:21.622 --> 00:10:25.778
That’s good south . . . Southern food and I was spoiled with that.

170
00:10:25.778 --> 00:10:28.044
Now, my mom could cook, too.

171
00:10:28.044 --> 00:10:29.933
These ladies were very good.

172
00:10:29.933 --> 00:10:35.222
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember anything about . . . Let’s see, you were graduating high school in ’73.

173
00:10:35.222 --> 00:10:36.356
<v MCMILLIN>Yes I did.

174
00:10:36.356 --> 00:10:39.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about integration?

175
00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:40.495
<v MCMILLIN>Well, let’s go back.

176
00:10:40.495 --> 00:10:42.363
We had some rough times.

177
00:10:42.363 --> 00:10:43.484
And there was

178
00:10:43.484 --> 00:10:45.725
<c.p>some people that pushed against us.

179
00:10:45.725 --> 00:10:53.198
I didn’t . . . I guess really I never saw color in my life because I was around it all my life.

180
00:10:53.198 --> 00:11:01.791
Black and white people to me, I never had any type of prejudice because I guess really I loved everybody, and still do.

181
00:11:01.791 --> 00:11:08.516
In junior high school we had policeman in our halls because it was starting to have some integration.

182
00:11:08.516 --> 00:11:10.385
<v ARSENEAULT>What junior high was it?

183
00:11:10.385 --> 00:11:10.758
<v MCMILLIN>S.P.

184
00:11:10.758 --> 00:11:12.253
Arnett Junior High School.

185
00:11:12.253 --> 00:11:13.000
<v ARSENEAULT>S.P. [. . .?]

186
00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:14.751
<v MCMILLIN>[?] Junior High School, in Westlake.

187
00:11:14.751 --> 00:11:15.102
Yeah.

188
00:11:15.102 --> 00:11:22.807
And they were just bringing in . . . They had Westlake blacks that lived in Westlake, and they had blacks that lived in Mossville.

189
00:11:22.807 --> 00:11:28.761
And they were bringing more blacks from Mossville to integrate Westlake Junior High and Westlake High School.

190
00:11:28.761 --> 00:11:30.863
And there was some racial tension.

191
00:11:30.863 --> 00:11:35.416
They had some negativity of people that didn’t want to see that happen.

192
00:11:35.416 --> 00:11:37.518
I remember some of those incidences.

193
00:11:37.518 --> 00:11:43.472
I saw some fights in the halls, I saw some police department folks having to be there.

194
00:11:43.472 --> 00:11:44.523
There was tension.

195
00:11:44.523 --> 00:11:50.477
But you could be a part of that tension, or not be a part of that tension.

196
00:11:50.477 --> 00:11:53.629
And I was never in the middle of that.

197
00:11:53.629 --> 00:11:58.883
It’s . . . Those were my friends and I guess I really didn’t get involved in it.

198
00:11:58.883 --> 00:12:06.939
I saw young white guys drive up to the school with rebel flags on their cars and kind of incite the black guys.

199
00:12:06.939 --> 00:12:15.345
And there were some black guys that were not happy to be there and didn’t want to be there, and they would push back.

200
00:12:15.345 --> 00:12:22.000
So it caused a tension in that era, but we got through that and it made us stronger.

201
00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:28.540
<c.p>Actually, on into high school, at Westlake High School, we had some of the same stuff when I got there.

202
00:12:28.540 --> 00:12:35.408
And in 1973 we had a lot of racial tension in Westlake with the integration that was going on and stuff.

203
00:12:35.408 --> 00:12:38.024
Our homecoming for the football game got canceled.

204
00:12:38.024 --> 00:12:43.583
And we didn’t have a homecoming queen in 1973 because there was racial tension in the air.

205
00:12:43.583 --> 00:12:45.545
But that was a few people.

206
00:12:45.545 --> 00:12:48.161
That was not the majority of the people.

207
00:12:48.161 --> 00:12:56.336
I can tell you the majority of people of Mossville and Westlake . . . like is say Ernest who worked for my dad as a plumber assistant,

208
00:12:56.336 --> 00:12:58.626
he was like a brother to me.

209
00:12:58.626 --> 00:13:00.915
We did a lot of duck hunting.

210
00:13:00.915 --> 00:13:06.801
And I’d have extra ducks, or geese, or whatever it was . . . I’d bring it to the Bates family.

211
00:13:06.801 --> 00:13:09.090
I was like part of the family.

212
00:13:09.090 --> 00:13:16.611
I never felt ill at ease going into anybody’s house, black or white, at any point in time like it was a problem.

213
00:13:16.611 --> 00:13:20.209
I guess there was people both sides, both white and black,

214
00:13:20.209 --> 00:13:25.768
that would . . . Could have problems with that in that era, but it didn’t affect the McMillin household.

215
00:13:25.768 --> 00:13:31.000
It wasn’t the way I was raised and it didn’t affect us in any way.

216
00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:34.250
<v ARSENEAULT>So you were raised kind of in that environment?

217
00:13:34.250 --> 00:13:37.861
<v MCMILLIN>I was raised in an environment that you respect people.

218
00:13:37.861 --> 00:13:41.833
<v ARSENEAULT>What other values did your parents kind of instill in you?

219
00:13:41.833 --> 00:13:44.000
<v MCMILLIN>Well, they were just good people.

220
00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:46.167
They were down to earth people.

221
00:13:46.167 --> 00:13:50.500
Treat people the way you want to be treated; the golden rule.

222
00:13:50.500 --> 00:13:58.083
Do unto others . . . that was always . . . and we would go to church and I . . . little things that you think back on.

223
00:13:58.083 --> 00:14:03.500
Childhood . . . I remember as a little kid being a little bit . . . Probably I had ADHD.

224
00:14:03.500 --> 00:14:05.306
They didn’t know what it

225
00:14:05.306 --> 00:14:08.556
<c.p>was back then but I was a hyper kid.

226
00:14:08.556 --> 00:14:10.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You were active?

227
00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:10.200
<v MCMILLIN>I was active.

228
00:14:10.128 --> 00:14:11.106
I’d remember my dad just kind of taking my knuckles, and just kind of squeezing them, and just a little bit of pressure.

229
00:14:11.106 --> 00:14:11.306
<v ARSENEAULT>Was that his punishment?

230
00:14:11.277 --> 00:14:11.915
<v MCMILLIN>He’d just kind of calm me down, just to slow me down a little bit.

231
00:14:11.915 --> 00:14:12.115
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

232
00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:20.598
<v MCMILLIN>He’d just grab . . . and do that to my grandson every now and again to just say hey, calm down a little.

233
00:14:20.598 --> 00:14:23.874
Just to get my attention a little bit.

234
00:14:23.874 --> 00:14:25.921
He wasn’t a strict disciplinarian.

235
00:14:25.921 --> 00:14:28.378
He was a really good guy.

236
00:14:28.378 --> 00:14:38.205
In fact, my older brother was probably more the disciplinarian than my dad was, but he was a hard working guy that enjoyed life.

237
00:14:38.205 --> 00:14:42.709
And the things that he enjoyed crossed over into our values.

238
00:14:42.709 --> 00:14:48.441
He enjoyed . . . We had rabbit dogs and we’d go rabbit hunting almost every weekend.

239
00:14:48.441 --> 00:14:52.126
We’d go perch fishing around the salt water barrier.

240
00:14:52.126 --> 00:14:53.764
Catch tons of fish.

241
00:14:53.764 --> 00:14:55.811
He was my best friend.

242
00:14:55.811 --> 00:14:57.039
So that was . . .

243
00:14:57.039 --> 00:14:59.906
<v ARSENEAULT>So who took care of the discipline?

244
00:14:59.906 --> 00:15:04.000
It was mostly your mom did it and your . . .?

245
00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:04.972
<v MCMILLIN>I was probably . . .

246
00:15:04.972 --> 00:15:07.241
<v ARSENEAULT>You weren’t a bad child, I’m sure.

247
00:15:07.241 --> 00:15:08.862
<v MCMILLIN>I wasn’t a bad child.

248
00:15:08.862 --> 00:15:13.724
I wasn’t a bad child, but I didn’t have to be disciplined a whole lot.

249
00:15:13.724 --> 00:15:15.669
But I knew right from wrong.

250
00:15:15.669 --> 00:15:17.614
I was taught right from wrong.

251
00:15:17.614 --> 00:15:19.883
And did I do some wrong sometimes?

252
00:15:19.883 --> 00:15:20.207
Yeah.

253
00:15:20.207 --> 00:15:22.152
Yeah we all did growing up.

254
00:15:22.152 --> 00:15:25.717
I mean there’s things that you do you think, “Wow man.

255
00:15:25.717 --> 00:15:29.931
If I’d have got caught doing that I’d have probably got in trouble.”

256
00:15:29.931 --> 00:15:32.200
But that’s the growth things through there.

257
00:15:32.200 --> 00:15:36.414
But I guess really the core values too, is family’s important, number one.

258
00:15:36.414 --> 00:15:38.034
Our family was number one.

259
00:15:38.034 --> 00:15:40.628
And there was always . . . I always felt loved.

260
00:15:40.628 --> 00:15:42.897
Being the baby I was always spoiled.

261
00:15:42.897 --> 00:15:44.841
My mom would scratch my back.

262
00:15:44.841 --> 00:15:47.434
She was probably the world’s greatest back scratcher.

263
00:15:47.434 --> 00:15:50.028
So yeah the core values of growing up,

264
00:15:50.028 --> 00:15:55.862
I look back where I’m at in my life and what . . . the time that we grew up in.

265
00:15:55.862 --> 00:15:59.428
I wouldn’t want to ever grow up in any other era.

266
00:15:59.428 --> 00:16:00.400
It was perfect.

267
00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:03.966
I mean we . . . from the television shows to life in general.

268
00:16:03.966 --> 00:16:11.421
Getting on your bicycle and heading down to the ballfield, playing right there at the recreation center was two blocks from my house.

269
00:16:11.421 --> 00:16:13.041
We’d play little league ball.

270
00:16:13.041 --> 00:16:21.793
I mean we were so anxious to play we’d go help them rake the fields and line the fields to get ready for the game that night.

271
00:16:21.793 --> 00:16:24.710
And it was . . . the swimming pool was right there.

272
00:16:24.710 --> 00:16:31.841
We had a lady up at the recreation center, Ms. [Hoban?], she was like a second momma to us all up there.

273
00:16:31.841 --> 00:16:38.000
And she didn’t put up with . . . she didn’t tolerate any kind of racial issues at that recreation center.

274
00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:40.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So it was an integrated pool?

275
00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:40.298
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah.

276
00:16:40.298 --> 00:16:42.685
Integrated pool, integrated recreation center, integrated baseball team.

277
00:16:42.685 --> 00:16:46.266
In fact, one of my dear buddies he still talks about that.

278
00:16:46.266 --> 00:16:50.444
He says, “Hey man back when times were tough and there was integration things,”

279
00:16:50.444 --> 00:16:55.218
he said . . . he always says, “Hal,” he said, “you were a guy that never played that.”

280
00:16:55.218 --> 00:16:58.500
He said, “You were my always friend all the way through.”

281
00:16:58.500 --> 00:16:59.395
And I did.

282
00:16:59.395 --> 00:17:02.379
I mean I just . . . Just the way I was raised.

283
00:17:02.379 --> 00:17:05.065
There wasn’t black white issues at our house, so.

284
00:17:05.065 --> 00:17:09.540
<v ARSENEAULT>It sounds like your dad was kind of a role model to you as well.

285
00:17:09.540 --> 00:17:11.927
Did you have any other role models, or?

286
00:17:11.927 --> 00:17:13.121
<v MCMILLIN>Well my big brother.

287
00:17:13.121 --> 00:17:15.210
He was a hard working guy also.

288
00:17:15.210 --> 00:17:15.508
He . . .

289
00:17:15.508 --> 00:17:17.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What was his name?

290
00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:17.877
<v MCMILLIN>His name’s Mickey.

291
00:17:17.877 --> 00:17:19.046
<v ARSENEAULT>What did he do?

292
00:17:19.046 --> 00:17:22.846
<v MCMILLIN>He’s a . . . Went to McNeese, graduated in forestry, and but he was a . . .

293
00:17:22.846 --> 00:17:23.138
<v ARSENEAULT>Outdoorsman?

294
00:17:23.138 --> 00:17:23.431
<v MCMILLIN>Outdoorsman.

295
00:17:23.431 --> 00:17:26.938
He was a big hunter so when my dad wouldn’t take me

296
00:17:26.938 --> 00:17:29.569
<c.p>hunting I could always go with my big brother.

297
00:17:29.569 --> 00:17:36.000
Now what I didn’t realize until later in life, he was taking me so he could shoot my limit also [laughs].

298
00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:36.943
<v ARSENEAULT>He was taking your ducks?

299
00:17:36.943 --> 00:17:38.075
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah he was taking my ducks.

300
00:17:38.075 --> 00:17:39.019
He was shooting my ducks.

301
00:17:39.019 --> 00:17:40.528
<v ARSENEAULT>You were just there as a warm body?

302
00:17:40.528 --> 00:17:43.547
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah I was a warm body so he could kill a few more ducks [both laugh].

303
00:17:43.547 --> 00:17:44.302
<v ARSENEAULT>What is the limit?

304
00:17:44.302 --> 00:17:44.868
Has it changed?

305
00:17:44.868 --> 00:17:46.000
<v MCMILLIN>It changed through the years.

306
00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:47.099
<v ARSENEAULT>It changed through the . . .

307
00:17:47.099 --> 00:17:49.570
<v MCMILLIN>It always changes, yeah, but yeah I say that.

308
00:17:49.570 --> 00:17:51.768
I give him a hard time about that.

309
00:17:51.768 --> 00:17:52.317
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s funny.

310
00:17:52.317 --> 00:17:53.415
<v MCMILLIN>But he taught me.

311
00:17:53.415 --> 00:17:53.690
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

312
00:17:53.690 --> 00:17:56.437
<v MCMILLIN>He taught me a lot of things about the outdoors.

313
00:17:56.437 --> 00:17:58.634
I mean . . . and there’s no . . . especially neighborhood kids.

314
00:17:58.634 --> 00:18:08.796
We had a great neighborhood with a whole bunch of kids and I was right in the mix with all those guys from playing plastic ball and bat in the backyard to football and in somebody’s yard.

315
00:18:08.796 --> 00:18:10.169
We were always doing something.

316
00:18:10.169 --> 00:18:11.542
A core group of kids.

317
00:18:11.542 --> 00:18:17.310
In fact, one of my closest friends, a young man named Mark Moore, him and I are still very close today.

318
00:18:17.310 --> 00:18:18.408
We were raised together.

319
00:18:18.408 --> 00:18:21.155
And I was a bit of a scoundrel at times.

320
00:18:21.155 --> 00:18:25.000
We would . . . I say scoundrel, I was fun loving I was not afraid . . .

321
00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:25.286
<v ARSENEAULT>Mischievous?

322
00:18:25.286 --> 00:18:26.429
<v MCMILLIN>Well, to a degree.

323
00:18:26.429 --> 00:18:30.429
We’d . . . I’m fortunate I didn’t kill Mark because he was like my younger brother.

324
00:18:30.429 --> 00:18:33.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of things would y’all get into?

325
00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:36.398
<v MCMILLIN>Well I once shot a bow and arrow straight up in the air . . .

326
00:18:36.398 --> 00:18:37.443
<v ARSENEAULT>Landed in somebody’s eyeball?

327
00:18:37.443 --> 00:18:40.580
<v MCMILLIN>And the arrow . . . No, the arrow came very close to hitting him.

328
00:18:40.580 --> 00:18:42.670
I mean it missed him by two inches.

329
00:18:42.670 --> 00:18:44.239
He tells that story a lot.

330
00:18:44.239 --> 00:18:46.330
There was one incident when I was probably

331
00:18:46.330 --> 00:18:54.170
<c.p>ten and he was probably eight that . . . when the Santa Claus issue came up I . . . he was one of the first guys I went to visit with about it, so.

332
00:18:54.170 --> 00:18:56.000
<v ARSENEAULT>To tell him there was none?

333
00:18:56.000 --> 00:18:57.059
<v MCMILLIN>Well I think there is one.

334
00:18:57.059 --> 00:18:57.765
I’m not saying that.

335
00:18:57.765 --> 00:18:58.294
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, I see.

336
00:18:58.294 --> 00:18:59.882
<v MCMILLIN>I don’t want to go on record with that.

337
00:18:59.882 --> 00:19:00.412
There still is . . .

338
00:19:00.412 --> 00:19:01.471
<v ARSENEAULT>So there is a Santa Claus?

339
00:19:01.471 --> 00:19:02.000
<v MCMILLIN>Yes, absolutely.

340
00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:02.667
<v ARSENEAULT>I see.

341
00:19:02.667 --> 00:19:03.333
I see.

342
00:19:03.333 --> 00:19:06.333
<v MCMILLIN>[Laughs] But at ten years old you may have . . .

343
00:19:06.333 --> 00:19:08.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You may have think otherwise.

344
00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:08.667
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah so . . .

345
00:19:08.667 --> 00:19:10.000
<v ARSENEAULT>That is funny.

346
00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:10.319
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah.

347
00:19:10.319 --> 00:19:11.594
He’s a great guy.

348
00:19:11.594 --> 00:19:12.551
I love Mark.

349
00:19:12.551 --> 00:19:16.377
Yeah we have lasting friendships through the neighborhood and stuff like that.

350
00:19:16.377 --> 00:19:21.797
<v ARSENEAULT>So y’all played ball, you went to the swimming pool, and this is when you were ten?

351
00:19:21.797 --> 00:19:23.072
When you were young?

352
00:19:23.072 --> 00:19:23.391
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah.

353
00:19:23.391 --> 00:19:27.536
Yeah we played in each other’s houses, we . . . it was just a neighborhood.

354
00:19:27.536 --> 00:19:29.449
And I think about that today.

355
00:19:29.449 --> 00:19:32.000
Often times kids say, “There’s nothing to do.”

356
00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:40.290
I don’t ever remember in my lifetime as a kid growing up in Westlake in my neighborhood that I ever said there was nothing to do.

357
00:19:40.290 --> 00:19:46.029
And I guess I was the kid that was always coming up with ideas for things to do.

358
00:19:46.029 --> 00:19:48.899
We were . . . They paved the street in the 60s.

359
00:19:48.899 --> 00:19:50.493
I had a lemonade stand.

360
00:19:50.493 --> 00:19:51.768
I made a killing.

361
00:19:51.768 --> 00:19:55.913
I was always a bit of an entrepreneur in different things with that.

362
00:19:55.913 --> 00:20:01.971
We had a trolley in the backyard that we used to . . . it was kind of like a zip line.

363
00:20:01.971 --> 00:20:05.159
We did zip lines way before zip lines were popular.

364
00:20:05.159 --> 00:20:10.580
I would put on carnival shows and charge local kids five cents to get in or something.

365
00:20:10.580 --> 00:20:16.000
I was always coming up with some scheme, or idea, or something to do something, but . . .

366
00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:18.103
<v ARSENEAULT>There was no time to be bored?

367
00:20:18.103 --> 00:20:20.507
<v MCMILLIN>No. There was no time to be bored.

368
00:20:20.507 --> 00:20:22.309
We had a lot of fun.

369
00:20:22.309 --> 00:20:27.717
We were always . . . Mark was really mechanically inclined and he was really good with flying these . . . He used

370
00:20:27.717 --> 00:20:31.323
<c.p>to have these prop airplanes on a string that you would fly.

371
00:20:31.323 --> 00:20:36.731
And we would fly airplanes, we would do racecar sets, we would . . . There was always something to do.

372
00:20:36.731 --> 00:20:37.332
Play baseball.

373
00:20:37.332 --> 00:20:41.538
Pass many hours of plastic ball and bat in the backyard or homerun derby.

374
00:20:41.538 --> 00:20:42.439
My gosh, basketball.

375
00:20:42.439 --> 00:20:43.641
What . . . all the sports.

376
00:20:43.641 --> 00:20:46.946
Just according to what season it was, what we was doing.

377
00:20:46.946 --> 00:20:50.552
And then of course you’d get into hunting season and fishing season.

378
00:20:50.552 --> 00:20:51.453
We loved that.

379
00:20:51.453 --> 00:20:57.462
And growing up here in southwest Louisiana and having the opportunity to have all the rivers and lakes and stuff,

380
00:20:57.462 --> 00:21:01.969
as soon as we got old enough to get boats and stuff . . . I was blessed.

381
00:21:01.969 --> 00:21:05.874
I was . . . My dad was a successful plumbing contractor, and my mom worked.

382
00:21:05.874 --> 00:21:10.081
So when I turned fifteen years old they had just built a new house.

383
00:21:10.081 --> 00:21:12.484
And that’s a small town of Westlake, okay?

384
00:21:12.484 --> 00:21:17.592
They just built a new house and it had a swimming pool in the center of it.

385
00:21:17.592 --> 00:21:23.000
So we had a house with a swimming pool, I had my own car, had my own . . .

386
00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:23.717
<v ARSENEAULT>At fifteen?

387
00:21:23.717 --> 00:21:24.792
<v MCMILLIN>At fifteen, yeah.

388
00:21:24.792 --> 00:21:25.867
<v ARSENEAULT>What was it?

389
00:21:25.867 --> 00:21:30.883
<v MCMILLIN>It was a Pontiac Grand Am, nineteen . . . well I had a Dodge Carnet first,

390
00:21:30.883 --> 00:21:38.050
but then for . . . when I got to be a senior . . . that was kind of the most fun years 1972, ’73.

391
00:21:38.050 --> 00:21:41.633
But I had a 1973 Pontiac Grand Am, brand new.

392
00:21:41.633 --> 00:21:42.350
Great car.

393
00:21:42.350 --> 00:21:44.142
Wish I still had it.

394
00:21:44.142 --> 00:21:49.158
Wish I would have took care of it [laughs], but had a ski boat.

395
00:21:49.158 --> 00:21:52.025
We skied on the river all the time.

396
00:21:52.025 --> 00:21:53.458
Had a camping trailer.

397
00:21:53.458 --> 00:21:55.250
We had all the toys.

398
00:21:55.250 --> 00:22:00.625
But here’s one thing with me, although I had all that stuff, I was never

399
00:22:00.625 --> 00:22:02.417
<c.p>stingy with that stuff, okay?

400
00:22:02.417 --> 00:22:06.000
We always . . . I was always friendly, so this is . . .

401
00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:08.654
<v ARSENEAULT>You wanted everybody to be having a good time?

402
00:22:08.654 --> 00:22:08.949
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah.

403
00:22:08.949 --> 00:22:10.423
Everybody have a good time.

404
00:22:10.423 --> 00:22:16.026
So this is one of the things one of the teachers . . . a guy named Mr. [Opry?] once told me.

405
00:22:16.026 --> 00:22:21.628
And when I went to Westlake High School in 1971, because I had all these toys to play with,

406
00:22:21.628 --> 00:22:26.346
the senior class of 1971 kind of took this young sophomore and kind of adopted me.

407
00:22:26.346 --> 00:22:28.115
So I ran with the seniors.

408
00:22:28.115 --> 00:22:29.000
Because I . . .

409
00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:31.550
<v ARSENEAULT>So like, “Ooh we could go on the lake.”

410
00:22:31.550 --> 00:22:36.083
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah because we’ll go skiing and we can . . . Hey, Hal’s got a boat, Hal’s got this,

411
00:22:36.083 --> 00:22:41.183
Hal’s got a car, we can load up and go to the Astros games or whatever we’d do.

412
00:22:41.183 --> 00:22:42.317
So that was fun.

413
00:22:42.317 --> 00:22:49.400
So I remember him saying he said, “I got to Westlake in 1971,” he said, “I thought you were a senior and you didn’t graduate.”

414
00:22:49.400 --> 00:22:54.783
So in 1972 that group of guys, Larry [?] and the Steve [Germans?] and all them, we were all friends.

415
00:22:54.783 --> 00:22:57.900
We all did things, and in 1972 I didn’t graduate again.

416
00:22:57.900 --> 00:23:01.583
That guy said, “Man this kid must be a remedial student or something.”

417
00:23:01.583 --> 00:23:03.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You mean held back?

418
00:23:03.000 --> 00:23:03.227
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah.

419
00:23:03.227 --> 00:23:05.267
But I actually . . . My year to graduate was 1973.

420
00:23:05.267 --> 00:23:08.893
And finally when I got to be a senior in ’73 I hardly knew those guys.

421
00:23:08.893 --> 00:23:11.160
I had to get acquainted with the class of ’73.

422
00:23:11.160 --> 00:23:11.840
<v ARSENEAULT>Like, oh man.

423
00:23:11.840 --> 00:23:14.560
<v MCMILLIN>But we quickly . . . we got a bunch of good buddies and friends.

424
00:23:14.560 --> 00:23:15.013
In fact . . .

425
00:23:15.013 --> 00:23:20.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I was going to ask what you did for fun in high school, but it sounds like you just . . . outdoor stuff.

426
00:23:20.000 --> 00:23:23.634
<v MCMILLIN>Oh, we was . . . yeah we was . . . kind of the life of the party.

427
00:23:23.634 --> 00:23:25.311
We had a lot of fun.

428
00:23:25.311 --> 00:23:25.590
Yeah.

429
00:23:25.590 --> 00:23:30.621
We would go camping in Sam Houston State Park, we would water ski on the river every day.

430
00:23:30.621 --> 00:23:36.211
I mean we had a sixteen foot [Washata?] ski boat with a walk through windshield and a sixty horse Johnson.

431
00:23:36.211 --> 00:23:40.404
And got to be a pretty good water skier because we did it every day.

432
00:23:40.404 --> 00:23:43.758
We could slalom and had a lot of fun on the river.

433
00:23:43.758 --> 00:23:45.714
And had a lot of good times.

434
00:23:45.714 --> 00:23:47.950
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you ever see any gators out there?

435
00:23:47.950 --> 00:23:48.509
Just curious.

436
00:23:48.509 --> 00:23:49.627
<v MCMILLIN>Gators and snakes, yeah.

437
00:23:49.627 --> 00:23:56.056
It was a really funny story about a guy that . . . I thought it was a duck it was swimming with its head up.

438
00:23:56.056 --> 00:24:01.087
And I kind of skied over toward it, it was a great big water moccasin swimming the river.

439
00:24:01.087 --> 00:24:05.000
And the guy driving the boat slowed down, so when he slowed down . . .

440
00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:05.982
<v ARSENEAULT>You fell on your . . .?

441
00:24:05.982 --> 00:24:09.667
<v MCMILLIN>It was . . . I was starting to go back in the water and I’m screaming, “Go!

442
00:24:09.667 --> 00:24:09.912
<c.p>Go!

443
00:24:09.912 --> 00:24:10.158
Go!”

444
00:24:10.158 --> 00:24:15.807
And then he sees it’s a snake and he takes the boat and circles over it, takes a boat paddle kills the snake,

445
00:24:15.807 --> 00:24:21.702
puts the snake in the boat, picks it up on the boat paddle, throws it in the water, and I’m still in the water.

446
00:24:21.702 --> 00:24:22.930
I’m screaming at this guy.

447
00:24:22.930 --> 00:24:24.158
I still haven’t forgiving him.

448
00:24:24.158 --> 00:24:24.649
Gary Thibodaux.

449
00:24:24.649 --> 00:24:27.596
Every time I see him we talk about that snake incident [laughs].

450
00:24:27.596 --> 00:24:29.807
But yeah we had a blast in high school.

451
00:24:29.807 --> 00:24:30.053
It . . .

452
00:24:30.053 --> 00:24:31.526
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you do any extracurricular activities?

453
00:24:31.526 --> 00:24:33.000
Sports or anything like that?

454
00:24:33.000 --> 00:24:37.897
<v MCMILLIN>I actually ended up playing on the state champion First Baptist church league basketball team.

455
00:24:37.897 --> 00:24:39.529
And here’s the reason why.

456
00:24:39.529 --> 00:24:44.426
I went out for high school basketball and my hair about as long as his.

457
00:24:44.426 --> 00:24:46.712
A little bit long for 1971, ’72.

458
00:24:46.712 --> 00:24:48.344
<v ARSENEAULT>Was that the style then?

459
00:24:48.344 --> 00:24:52.262
<v MCMILLIN>It was the Beatles style that was coming . . . It was the 70s.

460
00:24:52.262 --> 00:24:56.832
So having . . . but the coaches of that era wanted you to be clean cut.

461
00:24:56.832 --> 00:25:03.362
And I went to basketball practice as a sophomore and Coach Billy [Coughlan’s?], who’s now a great friend of mine,

462
00:25:03.362 --> 00:25:11.197
Billy and I are close, but he looked at me and he said, “McMillin,” he said, “I want you to get that haircut, son.

463
00:25:11.197 --> 00:25:13.156
Have it done by practice tomorrow.”

464
00:25:13.156 --> 00:25:14.788
And I said, “Yes, sir.

465
00:25:14.788 --> 00:25:15.441
I will.”

466
00:25:15.441 --> 00:25:19.359
I went down to the local barbershop [Poussons?] and it was packed.

467
00:25:19.359 --> 00:25:25.562
And I wasn’t able to get my haircut because it . . . so I go back to practice the next day.

468
00:25:25.562 --> 00:25:27.194
And Billy [Coughlan?] says, “McMillin!”

469
00:25:27.194 --> 00:25:28.500
I said, “Yes, sir.”

470
00:25:28.500 --> 00:25:31.765
He said, “Didn’t I tell you to get that haircut?”

471
00:25:31.765 --> 00:25:33.071
I’m like, “Yes, sir.

472
00:25:33.071 --> 00:25:35.029
I’m going to get it cut.”

473
00:25:35.029 --> 00:25:41.232
I said, “I went to [Poussons?] and the line was long and . . .” I didn’t get to finish my statement.

474
00:25:41.232 --> 00:25:41.559
He

475
00:25:41.559 --> 00:25:43.518
<c.p>said, “McMillin, you’re off the team.”

476
00:25:43.518 --> 00:25:45.150
Made an example of me.

477
00:25:45.150 --> 00:25:51.679
So he kicked me off the team and well, I was heartbroken because I thought I was Pistol Pete Maravich.

478
00:25:51.679 --> 00:25:59.188
I was the next . . . So we went on and there were some others guys that couldn’t play for Coach Coughlan’s at that time.

479
00:25:59.188 --> 00:26:06.697
And we went to the First Baptist church . . . we all went to the First Baptist church and we all organized a church team.

480
00:26:06.697 --> 00:26:10.615
And we ended up winning the Baptist state championship with that team.

481
00:26:10.615 --> 00:26:16.818
And we scrimmaged a high school team and we were beating them when they stopped us from playing them.

482
00:26:16.818 --> 00:26:19.756
But we had a really good church basketball team.

483
00:26:19.756 --> 00:26:20.735
Now I’m sixty . . .

484
00:26:20.735 --> 00:26:24.000
<v ARSENEAULT>That would have been the ultimate revenge, wouldn’t it?

485
00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:26.082
<v MCMILLIN>Well but here’s the funny part.

486
00:26:26.082 --> 00:26:28.510
Coughlan’s and I have become great friends.

487
00:26:28.510 --> 00:26:31.633
Here I am at sixty years old completely bald.

488
00:26:31.633 --> 00:26:36.837
So every time he sees me he’s like, “McMillin you can play for me now.”

489
00:26:36.837 --> 00:26:39.265
He just thinks it’s the biggest joke.

490
00:26:39.265 --> 00:26:42.735
Like, “Coach, you kicked me off the high school team!”

491
00:26:42.735 --> 00:26:44.816
So it’s a . . . It’s just Westlake.

492
00:26:44.816 --> 00:26:46.204
That was just Westlake.

493
00:26:46.204 --> 00:26:47.939
In the way it was.

494
00:26:47.939 --> 00:26:49.327
But everything worked out.

495
00:26:49.327 --> 00:26:50.367
It sure did.

496
00:26:50.367 --> 00:26:51.408
Good, good people.

497
00:26:51.408 --> 00:26:52.796
We had great teachers.

498
00:26:52.796 --> 00:26:54.878
We had a great high school.

499
00:26:54.878 --> 00:26:55.571
I was . . .

500
00:26:55.571 --> 00:26:58.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have a favorite teacher?

501
00:26:58.000 --> 00:27:01.130
<v MCMILLIN>The favorite teacher . . . There were so many great teachers.

502
00:27:01.130 --> 00:27:06.696
I tell you, I was an avid hunter and probably some of this I shouldn’t share,

503
00:27:06.696 --> 00:27:11.565
but I guess I will because most of them are passed away and gone.

504
00:27:11.565 --> 00:27:14.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You skipped class to go hunting?

505
00:27:14.000 --> 00:27:14.594
<v MCMILLIN>Oh, yeah.

506
00:27:14.594 --> 00:27:15.782
Yeah lots of times.

507
00:27:15.782 --> 00:27:22.611
We would go early in the morning prior to homeroom and a lot of times we wouldn’t make it into class on time.

508
00:27:22.611 --> 00:27:27.659
But if you would bring the teachers the ducks, they kind of waived that tardy slip [laughs].

509
00:27:27.659 --> 00:27:34.785
So we learned pretty quick that okay we can make a hunting trip and bring so-and-so some ducks and we’re going to be okay.

510
00:27:34.785 --> 00:27:38.645
And probably my grade point average might have been on a curve, too.

511
00:27:38.645 --> 00:27:48.444
Because I could have been . . . in hindsight I wish I’d really studied and I wish I had really would applied myself in high school because I could have been a really good student.

512
00:27:48.444 --> 00:27:50.819
But it was a . . . I played a lot.

513
00:27:50.819 --> 00:27:54.976
I had a blast, and we hunted a lot, we had a good time,

514
00:27:54.976 --> 00:27:58.242
and . . . Yeah, but now as I see my kids and grandkids,

515
00:27:58.242 --> 00:28:08.635
school was a place to prepare for an education to get a job that you don’t have to work so hard in life and you can make all the money that you want to have.

516
00:28:08.635 --> 00:28:12.495
And of course, one of the things I call money is fun coupons.

517
00:28:12.495 --> 00:28:15.761
The more money you have, the more fun you can have.

518
00:28:15.761 --> 00:28:19.621
So if somebody would have taught me at an early age, “Hey man,

519
00:28:19.621 --> 00:28:24.966
get that education so you can have fun coupons so you can go on those trips to Jamaica,

520
00:28:24.966 --> 00:28:28.529
or to snow skiing in Colorado, and you can afford all that.”

521
00:28:28.529 --> 00:28:32.092
I try to share that sometimes with kids when I see them.

522
00:28:32.092 --> 00:28:34.765
Say, “Look, most important things is get the education.”

523
00:28:34.765 --> 00:28:39.515
And when I find the class clown in the class, I’m especially drawn to that kid.

524
00:28:39.515 --> 00:28:41.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Because that was you?

525
00:28:41.000 --> 00:28:42.120
<v MCMILLIN>That was me.

526
00:28:42.120 --> 00:28:42.493
Yeah.

527
00:28:42.493 --> 00:28:43.987
I always had fun.

528
00:28:43.987 --> 00:28:46.227
We always had a good time.

529
00:28:46.227 --> 00:28:48.467
We always laughed and cut up.

530
00:28:48.467 --> 00:28:52.200
And I guess you can . . . sometimes . . . and then things and

531
00:28:52.200 --> 00:28:58.547
<c.p>whether the job I have now as a business development manager, or whether . . . It’s about building relationships.

532
00:28:58.547 --> 00:29:02.280
And you can build a lot of relationships with humor.

533
00:29:02.280 --> 00:29:06.760
And when people see that laughing . . . I laugh at myself a lot.

534
00:29:06.760 --> 00:29:10.120
I mean I’m . . . I think I do funny things.

535
00:29:10.120 --> 00:29:13.853
And when I do something funny I laugh about it.

536
00:29:13.853 --> 00:29:20.573
Then when . . . That really diffuses a situation when people see that you can laugh at your own self.

537
00:29:20.573 --> 00:29:23.187
That you’re not taking yourself so serious.

538
00:29:23.187 --> 00:29:24.680
It’s . . . It really helps.

539
00:29:24.680 --> 00:29:25.427
Humor is . . .

540
00:29:25.427 --> 00:29:28.040
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you learn that from your family?

541
00:29:28.040 --> 00:29:29.907
Or is that just something . . .?

542
00:29:29.907 --> 00:29:34.760
<v MCMILLIN>I guess that’s just something that I learned through life just watching people.

543
00:29:34.760 --> 00:29:36.253
I’ve always watched people.

544
00:29:36.253 --> 00:29:39.987
And so I guess as I’m . . . I’ve always impersonated people.

545
00:29:39.987 --> 00:29:47.453
And whether it was local people that were a little different or a couple of impersonations . . . Forrest Gump and Elvis.

546
00:29:47.453 --> 00:29:54.920
It’s kind of . . . through that impersonation though, of watching things and doing things, it opened up a career for me.

547
00:29:54.920 --> 00:30:00.147
As a young kid growing up, we would go to auctions on Friday nights.

548
00:30:00.147 --> 00:30:02.013
That was a big event.

549
00:30:02.013 --> 00:30:05.000
And I would always impersonate the auctioneer.

550
00:30:05.000 --> 00:30:06.154
<v ARSENEAULT>The cattle auction?

551
00:30:06.154 --> 00:30:09.231
<v MCMILLIN>It was a . . . brown furniture they call it.

552
00:30:09.231 --> 00:30:10.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

553
00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:11.493
<v MCMILLIN>Kind of junk auctions.

554
00:30:11.493 --> 00:30:13.731
It was all kind of stuff.

555
00:30:13.731 --> 00:30:14.851
They would have

556
00:30:14.851 --> 00:30:19.328
<c.p>furniture, and washing machines, and everything from ax, hatchets, sledgehammers, toys, whatever.

557
00:30:19.328 --> 00:30:20.821
It was Morris’ Auction.

558
00:30:20.821 --> 00:30:22.687
Dave Black was the auctioneer.

559
00:30:22.687 --> 00:30:27.537
And we were . . . Friday night that was what we did as a family.

560
00:30:27.537 --> 00:30:32.761
We’d go to the auction and no telling what we might come home with.

561
00:30:32.761 --> 00:30:35.746
But due to that, and impersonating the auctioneer . . .

562
00:30:35.746 --> 00:30:38.358
<v ARSENEAULT>They’re the ones that talk really fast?

563
00:30:38.358 --> 00:30:38.731
<v MCMILLIN>Yes.

564
00:30:38.731 --> 00:30:45.821
I had a friend that had cancer and she needed an auctioneer and I did the auction for her.

565
00:30:45.821 --> 00:30:58.507
Well in 2007, I decided I wanted to get my auctioneer license so I went to Texas Auctioneer Academy and I’ve been a licensed and bonded auctioneer in the state of Louisiana since 2007.

566
00:30:58.507 --> 00:30:58.881
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

567
00:30:58.881 --> 00:31:00.000
That’s awesome.

568
00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:00.297
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah.

569
00:31:00.297 --> 00:31:00.891
It’s wonderful.

570
00:31:00.891 --> 00:31:02.376
<v ARSENEAULT>Kind of strange how life . . .

571
00:31:02.376 --> 00:31:06.832
<v MCMILLIN>See and then that really combines all of things that . . . That’s my gift from God.

572
00:31:06.832 --> 00:31:10.990
It really is because I can give that back to a lot of people.

573
00:31:10.990 --> 00:31:14.554
I can raise a lot of money for a lot of people.

574
00:31:14.554 --> 00:31:19.307
That’s a gift that . . . One I can make money at, and one I can give back.

575
00:31:19.307 --> 00:31:19.604
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

576
00:31:19.604 --> 00:31:20.792
<v MCMILLIAN>So it’s . . . that’s something.

577
00:31:20.792 --> 00:31:29.109
And hopefully someday . . . my wife Sue also went to auctioneer school after I went to learn the business side of it . . . That we’ll have our own auction business,

578
00:31:29.109 --> 00:31:29.406
but . . .

579
00:31:29.406 --> 00:31:30.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Awesome.

580
00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:30.300
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah.

581
00:31:30.300 --> 00:31:31.200
That’s pretty neat.

582
00:31:31.200 --> 00:31:34.200
<v ARSENEAULT>So you went to high school, you somehow made it . . .

583
00:31:34.200 --> 00:31:35.100
<v MCMILLIN>I made it.

584
00:31:35.100 --> 00:31:36.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And then . . .

585
00:31:36.000 --> 00:31:37.778
<v MCMILLIN>I went to McNeese.

586
00:31:37.778 --> 00:31:38.222
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

587
00:31:38.222 --> 00:31:38.667
<v MCMILLIN>In . . .

588
00:31:38.667 --> 00:31:39.111
<v ARSENEAULT>’73?

589
00:31:39.111 --> 00:31:40.000
Or . . .

590
00:31:40.000 --> 00:31:42.892
<v MCMILLIN>Seventy . . . I graduated in ’73, so ’74, ’75.

591
00:31:42.892 --> 00:31:44.337
A couple years at

592
00:31:44.337 --> 00:31:44.699
<c.p>McNeese.

593
00:31:44.699 --> 00:31:47.952
I was going to school to be a coach.

594
00:31:47.952 --> 00:31:55.542
Although getting kicked off the basketball team didn’t hinder me I thought, hey I want to be a middle school coach.

595
00:31:55.542 --> 00:31:58.795
And that was . . . so you . . . I was in education.

596
00:31:58.795 --> 00:32:03.133
But what I didn’t really realize is how much they got paid.

597
00:32:03.133 --> 00:32:11.446
And when I started looking at what the salary was of these, I said, “Wow maybe this isn’t the right opportunity for me.”

598
00:32:11.446 --> 00:32:17.952
So I actually had an opportunity to go put in an application at one of the local industries.

599
00:32:17.952 --> 00:32:21.566
And Westlake being the hometown bedroom community to the industries,

600
00:32:21.566 --> 00:32:28.434
suddenly I know all the people that are in this plant because one of them was my baseball coach,

601
00:32:28.434 --> 00:32:32.771
and another one, my dad and him are best friends and so-and-so.

602
00:32:32.771 --> 00:32:40.000
So I went and applied at Conoco Chemical and I ended up getting a job . . . A really good job.

603
00:32:40.000 --> 00:32:41.136
<v ARSENEAULT>And you say the bedroom.

604
00:32:41.136 --> 00:32:44.091
Does that mean like everyone that works at the plant lives in Westlake?

605
00:32:44.091 --> 00:32:44.318
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah.

606
00:32:44.318 --> 00:32:45.000
Bedroom community.

607
00:32:45.000 --> 00:32:45.317
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

608
00:32:45.317 --> 00:32:48.173
<v MCMILLIN>Westlake was always a bedroom community to the industries.

609
00:32:48.173 --> 00:32:53.250
The lion’s share of the people that lived in Westlake and Mossville worked in either Conoco,

610
00:32:53.250 --> 00:32:57.375
or Conoco Chemical at the time, or maybe Citgo and stuff like this.

611
00:32:57.375 --> 00:33:00.865
So Westlake was known as a bedroom community to the industries.

612
00:33:00.865 --> 00:33:07.846
So I ended up not finishing McNeese because I found a job that instead of making fourteen to sixteen thousand a year,

613
00:33:07.846 --> 00:33:11.019
started me off twenty five to thirty thousand a year.

614
00:33:11.019 --> 00:33:13.875
<v ARSENEAULT>You’d be kind of foolish not to I think.

615
00:33:13.875 --> 00:33:14.192
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah.

616
00:33:14.192 --> 00:33:17.365
Yeah so that’s where my career started in the industries.

617
00:33:17.365 --> 00:33:18.000
And . . .

618
00:33:18.000 --> 00:33:19.632
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about McNeese?

619
00:33:19.632 --> 00:33:22.079
So you were there for a couple of years.

620
00:33:22.079 --> 00:33:23.439
Did you live on campus?

621
00:33:23.439 --> 00:33:24.798
<v MCMILLIN>No, I lived at home.

622
00:33:24.798 --> 00:33:30.509
Drove back and forth carpool with a girl that I graduated my high school with, which was the worst driver ever.

623
00:33:30.509 --> 00:33:32.956
She would drive down . . . We still laugh about that.

624
00:33:32.956 --> 00:33:37.035
Glenda Carson, but Glenda [Rampant?] and later her dad was one of my top operators.

625
00:33:37.035 --> 00:33:39.211
But my gosh, carpooling to school with her.

626
00:33:39.211 --> 00:33:40.570
We had fun at McNeese.

627
00:33:40.570 --> 00:33:41.386
It was good.

628
00:33:41.386 --> 00:33:42.474
We had good classes.

629
00:33:42.474 --> 00:33:43.561
I was doing well.

630
00:33:43.561 --> 00:33:47.640
I started kind of applying myself because I had to study because they didn’t care . . .

631
00:33:47.640 --> 00:33:49.000
<v ARSENEAULT>They didn’t accept ducks?

632
00:33:49.000 --> 00:33:49.358
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah.

633
00:33:49.358 --> 00:33:50.789
They didn’t accept ducks.

634
00:33:50.789 --> 00:33:53.652
Well, maybe a teacher or two did [laughs].

635
00:33:53.652 --> 00:34:00.093
But it didn’t work on . . . yeah they didn’t accept ducks so I had to really kind of study.

636
00:34:00.093 --> 00:34:03.672
And it was fun the couple years I was there.

637
00:34:03.672 --> 00:34:05.819
We were having a good time.

638
00:34:05.819 --> 00:34:12.260
But the realization of that . . . Hey what I want to do doesn’t pay much money, my life changed.

639
00:34:12.260 --> 00:34:17.270
And then when you started looking at the industries . . . and look, I’m very pro-industry.

640
00:34:17.270 --> 00:34:25.500
I grew up in the industry so it’s always been a part of my community, it’s always been a part of my life.

641
00:34:25.500 --> 00:34:32.657
So getting on at the industries and finding out that, hey you’ve got a very good salary, you’ve got a

642
00:34:32.657 --> 00:34:36.951
<c.p>401K, you’ve got healthcare, I mean its . . . it was good to me.

643
00:34:36.951 --> 00:34:43.750
Though I’ll say baseball was very very good to me, the industries are very very good to my family.

644
00:34:43.750 --> 00:34:50.191
And of course my dad being a plumber built many houses for people that worked in the industries.

645
00:34:50.191 --> 00:34:53.054
Whether it was Conoco, Conoco Chemical, Citgo, whatever.

646
00:34:53.054 --> 00:35:02.000
These were good jobs and that started my industrial career out there as eighteen years in operations, and five years in the environmental group.

647
00:35:02.000 --> 00:35:03.514
<v ARSENEAULT>What made you choose Conoco?

648
00:35:03.514 --> 00:35:05.634
Because I know there’s a bunch of . . .

649
00:35:05.634 --> 00:35:09.268
<v MCMILLIN>Well I’d actually got on . . . I had a couple of applications in.

650
00:35:09.268 --> 00:35:14.415
I had one out at PPG, and Conoco was the first that called me in . . . tested me.

651
00:35:14.415 --> 00:35:18.958
I passed the test and they made me a job offer and I took it.

652
00:35:18.958 --> 00:35:23.500
A week later PPG called me and said, “Hey we got a job for you.”

653
00:35:23.500 --> 00:35:24.711
I said, “I’m sorry.

654
00:35:24.711 --> 00:35:26.831
I’m already working at Conoco, thank you.”

655
00:35:26.831 --> 00:35:29.254
But yeah it was . . . I guess God’s intervention.

656
00:35:29.254 --> 00:35:32.887
And I worked at the chemical plant right there at Conoco Chemical,

657
00:35:32.887 --> 00:35:38.338
what is now . . . It was Conoco Chemical, then it went to Vista, Condea Vista, and now it’s Sasol.

658
00:35:38.338 --> 00:35:40.155
So I grew up out there.

659
00:35:40.155 --> 00:35:42.880
I got on when I was twenty years old.

660
00:35:42.880 --> 00:35:45.000
And a twenty year old kid.

661
00:35:45.000 --> 00:35:46.651
<v ARSENEAULT>What were your first duties?

662
00:35:46.651 --> 00:35:47.972
You were an operator?

663
00:35:47.972 --> 00:35:49.623
<v MCMILLIN>First duties were utility helper.

664
00:35:49.623 --> 00:35:53.255
I was a grunt guy and they . . . Look, they torture you.

665
00:35:53.255 --> 00:35:58.868
That was in 1976 when I started at Conoco Chemical as a young, know it all twenty,

666
00:35:58.868 --> 00:36:05.472
twenty-one year old kid coming into a world of thirty, forty, fifty year old guys that had lot of experience.

667
00:36:05.472 --> 00:36:11.415
And you know you often hear the statement I wish I knew what I knew now, well I’d

668
00:36:11.415 --> 00:36:12.736
<c.p>hear them say that.

669
00:36:12.736 --> 00:36:15.377
I’d say, “Well tell me what you know.

670
00:36:15.377 --> 00:36:16.038
Come on.

671
00:36:16.038 --> 00:36:16.698
Tell me.”

672
00:36:16.698 --> 00:36:20.000
Said, “Well you’re just going to have to learn.”

673
00:36:20.000 --> 00:36:21.253
<v ARSENEAULT>Figure it out?

674
00:36:21.253 --> 00:36:29.610
<v MCMILLIN>Now I know . . . At sixty I know what I know now, but at twenty I thought I knew it all.

675
00:36:29.610 --> 00:36:30.027
And . . .

676
00:36:30.027 --> 00:36:31.281
<v ARSENEAULT>What was it?

677
00:36:31.281 --> 00:36:33.788
What do you know Mr. Hal?

678
00:36:33.788 --> 00:36:39.637
<v MCMILLIN>Well it’s . . . the true life experiences of what it takes to get something done.

679
00:36:39.637 --> 00:36:43.397
Your individualness is not going to get you through.

680
00:36:43.397 --> 00:36:51.336
You really need . . . I guess really you need to depend on your life lessons that you have to learn,

681
00:36:51.336 --> 00:37:01.363
to become much more mellower, and to listen to people, to empower people to help you, to not try to do it all yourself.

682
00:37:01.363 --> 00:37:03.034
Some of those things.

683
00:37:03.034 --> 00:37:05.959
That you think at twenty, “I’m invincible.”

684
00:37:05.959 --> 00:37:15.986
But you realize later in life that hey, you’re not invincible and that if you utilize the other people’s great skills that they have,

685
00:37:15.986 --> 00:37:21.000
you can be so much stronger and do so much more.

686
00:37:21.000 --> 00:37:23.082
<v ARSENEAULT>So how did they torture you?

687
00:37:23.082 --> 00:37:26.206
<v MCMILLIN>Oh, they would . . . every little dirty job there was.

688
00:37:26.206 --> 00:37:35.229
The first time I worked overtime we had to chip some [coke?] out of this big pipe and then we had to clean this convection section.

689
00:37:35.229 --> 00:37:38.353
And this guy was like, “I’ll do the chipping.

690
00:37:38.353 --> 00:37:39.741
I’ll do the chipping.

691
00:37:39.741 --> 00:37:40.435
I’m going

692
00:37:40.435 --> 00:37:43.906
<c.p>to let you do the cleaning of the convection section.”

693
00:37:43.906 --> 00:37:53.624
Well what I didn’t know was that I had to put on a mask and a paper suit and get in and blow this dust out of there.

694
00:37:53.624 --> 00:37:58.482
And when I came home from work I had to take like three baths.

695
00:37:58.482 --> 00:38:04.382
I was completely orange with this . . . Some of this powder and dust that I had back then.

696
00:38:04.382 --> 00:38:08.200
But they fed me, and I’ll never forget my first check.

697
00:38:08.200 --> 00:38:16.876
Because I wasn’t used to making a lot of money, but my first check was like 856 dollars for a week or two weeks work.

698
00:38:16.876 --> 00:38:20.000
And I’m like, “My God I’m rich.” [Laughs].

699
00:38:20.000 --> 00:38:21.615
<v ARSENEAULT>What’d you do with the money?

700
00:38:21.615 --> 00:38:22.423
Do you remember?

701
00:38:22.423 --> 00:38:25.115
<v MCMILLIN>I was living . . . well I was living in a trailer.

702
00:38:25.115 --> 00:38:25.923
I was married.

703
00:38:25.923 --> 00:38:29.423
It was a first marriage and I was just like, “Look we’re rich.”

704
00:38:29.423 --> 00:38:36.154
And I started signing up working all the overtime I could because . . . And then I found that there were more cleaner jobs than that one,

705
00:38:36.154 --> 00:38:39.115
but I kind of got hit with that one right off.

706
00:38:39.115 --> 00:38:40.462
And they’d pick at you.

707
00:38:40.462 --> 00:38:45.846
Send you after a left-handed wrench, which there’s no such thing as a left-handed wrench, or a bucket of steam.

708
00:38:45.846 --> 00:38:47.192
Just general stuff like that.

709
00:38:47.192 --> 00:38:48.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Just hazing.

710
00:38:48.000 --> 00:38:48.703
<v MCMILLIN>Hazing, yeah.

711
00:38:48.703 --> 00:38:50.108
Kind of the hazing.

712
00:38:50.108 --> 00:38:51.514
But it was good.

713
00:38:51.514 --> 00:38:54.324
It was such a great group of guys.

714
00:38:54.324 --> 00:38:58.541
They never hurt you, but they would push you to the edge.

715
00:38:58.541 --> 00:38:59.595
<v ARSENEAULT>They would joke.

716
00:38:59.595 --> 00:39:01.351
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah they were practical jokers.

717
00:39:01.351 --> 00:39:09.081
And I guess really you learned real quick not to let them . . . Not to let anything they said or did bother you.

718
00:39:09.081 --> 00:39:11.541
Because if they saw it bother you . . .

719
00:39:11.541 --> 00:39:14.000
<v ARSENEAULT>They’re going to keep doing it.

720
00:39:14.000 --> 00:39:15.695
<v MCMILLIN>They were going to torture you.

721
00:39:15.695 --> 00:39:21.344
So its . . . one heater, two bleeder, fireball McMillin give me all the nicknames you want because it don’t bother me.

722
00:39:21.344 --> 00:39:22.756
Put it on my hardhat.

723
00:39:22.756 --> 00:39:23.603
And it’s . . . yeah.

724
00:39:23.603 --> 00:39:27.840
People would try to mess with me or pick on me I just . . . Man, look.

725
00:39:27.840 --> 00:39:29.817
I was an operator for eighteen years.

726
00:39:29.817 --> 00:39:31.511
You’re not going to get me.

727
00:39:31.511 --> 00:39:34.336
I’ve been through some of the guys I work with.

728
00:39:34.336 --> 00:39:41.679
I had a guy that I used to work with in one of the unit, he would take two weeks to set up a practical joke.

729
00:39:41.679 --> 00:39:43.374
I mean this guy was calculated.

730
00:39:43.374 --> 00:39:46.481
Then he would pull it on you and you’re like, “Wow.”

731
00:39:46.481 --> 00:39:47.611
<v ARSENEAULT>Didn’t see it coming?

732
00:39:47.611 --> 00:39:48.740
<v MCMILLIN>Didn’t see it coming.

733
00:39:48.740 --> 00:39:51.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember any of them, or?

734
00:39:51.000 --> 00:39:51.356
<v MCMILLIN>No.

735
00:39:51.356 --> 00:39:52.426
<v ARSENEAULT>You just remember . . .

736
00:39:52.426 --> 00:39:54.921
<v MCMILLIN>We just had a lot of fun.

737
00:39:54.921 --> 00:39:55.277
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

738
00:39:55.277 --> 00:39:55.634
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah.

739
00:39:55.634 --> 00:39:58.129
The industries have changed a whole lot.

740
00:39:58.129 --> 00:40:02.050
They’re a lot stricter than when we first got out there.

741
00:40:02.050 --> 00:40:05.970
I mean they were . . . it was . . . there was definitely a protocol.

742
00:40:05.970 --> 00:40:09.535
And it’s one of the things you learned out there.

743
00:40:09.535 --> 00:40:16.307
That you had the shift foremans who were the bosses, you had the top operators who you listened to,

744
00:40:16.307 --> 00:40:20.941
you had the boardmen who would give you orders, and you were outside.

745
00:40:20.941 --> 00:40:28.069
And as you progressed up in those places, it was like a rank and you would do as they said.

746
00:40:28.069 --> 00:40:37.693
And our mission every shift we had was to get there and make [on spec product?] and do the best that we could to keep everybody safe,

747
00:40:37.693 --> 00:40:40.545
and running right, and make [on spec?] product.

748
00:40:40.545 --> 00:40:41.970
But here’s the deal.

749
00:40:41.970 --> 00:40:50.525
In the twenty-three years we were out there, I keep seeing these guys come in with boudin sausage or bringing in pistolettes, or donuts.

750
00:40:50.525 --> 00:40:52.307
I’m like, “What’s your job?”

751
00:40:52.307 --> 00:40:53.020
“Business development.”

752
00:40:53.020 --> 00:40:54.446
“What do you do?”

753
00:40:54.446 --> 00:40:58.366
“Build relationships so our company can get some work out here.”

754
00:40:58.366 --> 00:41:00.861
I’m going to have that job someday.

755
00:41:00.861 --> 00:41:02.287
<v ARSENEAULT>Eat boudin all day?

756
00:41:02.287 --> 00:41:03.000
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah.

757
00:41:03.000 --> 00:41:04.636
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s the job to have.

758
00:41:04.636 --> 00:41:07.255
<v MCMILLIN>That’s what I had to do this morning.

759
00:41:07.255 --> 00:41:09.873
I had to go deliver boudin for you

760
00:41:09.873 --> 00:41:10.527
<c.p>out here.

761
00:41:10.527 --> 00:41:18.382
Boudin sausage to . . . so I’m a business . . . My real job now is I’m a business development manager for an engineering company for Levingston Engineers.

762
00:41:18.382 --> 00:41:21.000
I’ve been doing that for about ten years.

763
00:41:21.000 --> 00:41:24.273
But I worked from 1976 to 1999 in the industries.

764
00:41:24.273 --> 00:41:27.873
Then I went into business development with a number of companies.

765
00:41:27.873 --> 00:41:35.727
Market kind of slowed, went to some different companies, but I’ve locked in with this company that I’ve been with now for ten years.

766
00:41:35.727 --> 00:41:36.382
Great job.

767
00:41:36.382 --> 00:41:37.036
Great job.

768
00:41:37.036 --> 00:41:44.564
Great people, great job, so I get . . . although I don’t, as you remember back, don’t have that college degree, I have a gift.

769
00:41:44.564 --> 00:41:48.491
And my gift is being a people person, being a good listener,

770
00:41:48.491 --> 00:41:54.709
understanding through all the hazing and harassment that I’ve gotten in my life, to not get upset about something.

771
00:41:54.709 --> 00:41:56.673
To stay low-key, mellow, and listen.

772
00:41:56.673 --> 00:42:03.873
And through building relationships, it makes my job, what I do as a business development manager for an engineering company, really good.

773
00:42:03.873 --> 00:42:08.455
I’ve got really good . . . I sell the work and skill of engineers, good engineers.

774
00:42:08.455 --> 00:42:15.000
And I’ve learned what they do and I don’t have to do it, but I know what they do.

775
00:42:15.000 --> 00:42:18.553
<v ARSENEAULT>They might not be able to communicate what they do, but you can for them?

776
00:42:18.553 --> 00:42:21.395
<v MCMILLIN>I wasn’t going to say that, but being how you said that . . .

777
00:42:21.395 --> 00:42:22.105
<v ARSENEAULT>I know engineers.

778
00:42:22.105 --> 00:42:23.526
<v MCMILLIN>Engineers at times can be analytical.

779
00:42:23.526 --> 00:42:24.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Right.

780
00:42:24.000 --> 00:42:26.083
<v MCMILLIN>So they need a personality.

781
00:42:26.083 --> 00:42:26.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Right.

782
00:42:26.500 --> 00:42:28.167
<v MCMILLIN>Well, here I am.

783
00:42:28.167 --> 00:42:29.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Right.

784
00:42:29.000 --> 00:42:32.922
<v MCMILLIN>So the things that I do keep me in the public eye, okay?

785
00:42:32.922 --> 00:42:40.164
Many years ago I started doing a radio show in 1995 and from that radio show came an opportunity to do television outdoor tips.

786
00:42:40.164 --> 00:42:41.672
<v ARSENEAULT>What is the radio show?

787
00:42:41.672 --> 00:42:43.784
<v MCMILLIN>The radio show is called Sportsman’s Paradise.

788
00:42:43.784 --> 00:42:47.707
I do it once a week on Wednesday afternoons from five to six.

789
00:42:47.707 --> 00:42:50.121
We talk about hunting, fishing, hiking, biking, camping,

790
00:42:50.121 --> 00:42:58.871
all the things southwest Louisiana . . . on today’s show I’m going to have a girl there . . . we got the whitetail deer auction coming up and we were talking about that.

791
00:42:58.871 --> 00:43:05.509
Last week’s show we were talking about salty catch fishing rodeo, before that . . . the week before the fourth of July fishing rodeo.

792
00:43:05.509 --> 00:43:07.621
So I do a weekly radio show.

793
00:43:07.621 --> 00:43:11.543
Once a month I go on KPLC and I do the outdoor tips.

794
00:43:11.543 --> 00:43:18.784
Being a hunting guide, that I started back in the 70s, I was a hunting guide from the early 70s for about thirty years.

795
00:43:18.784 --> 00:43:21.802
All this has laid the groundwork for what we do.

796
00:43:21.802 --> 00:43:24.517
And all that’s laid the groundwork for business development.

797
00:43:24.517 --> 00:43:26.629
Sometimes in business development you hunt with

798
00:43:26.629 --> 00:43:28.741
<c.p>people, sometimes you play golf with them.

799
00:43:28.741 --> 00:43:29.948
I enjoy playing golf.

800
00:43:29.948 --> 00:43:32.362
Sometimes you build the relationships in different ways.

801
00:43:32.362 --> 00:43:33.267
Avid LSU fan.

802
00:43:33.267 --> 00:43:37.491
You might see me at an LSU game, or a tailgate party or something.

803
00:43:37.491 --> 00:43:46.543
So there’s a lot of things that I’ve done in life to get me to what my job is now as . . . My true job as business development manager for Levingston.

804
00:43:46.543 --> 00:43:55.595
The auction stuff doesn’t hurt because it exposes me that I’m in a crowd of four hundred people and a lot of these people are industrial people and they say,

805
00:43:55.595 --> 00:43:57.405
“Hey that’s Hal McMillin up there.”

806
00:43:57.405 --> 00:44:04.948
And so when I go in their plant we talk about the different things maybe where there’s an auction, maybe where the fish are biting.

807
00:44:04.948 --> 00:44:09.776
Different things, or LSU, or McNeese, or whatever that you can build that rapport with them.

808
00:44:09.776 --> 00:44:10.983
So that works out.

809
00:44:10.983 --> 00:44:12.190
And I’m having fun.

810
00:44:12.190 --> 00:44:14.000
I got one of the . . .

811
00:44:14.000 --> 00:44:14.235
<v ARSENEAULT>Good.

812
00:44:14.235 --> 00:44:16.118
<v MCMILLIN>My wife says I don’t have a job.

813
00:44:16.118 --> 00:44:17.529
I said, “Yes I do, baby.

814
00:44:17.529 --> 00:44:18.941
They pay me every two weeks.”

815
00:44:18.941 --> 00:44:22.000
<v ARSENEAULT>She says you’re having too much fun for it to be work?

816
00:44:22.000 --> 00:44:23.349
<v MCMILLIN>I’m having too much fun.

817
00:44:23.349 --> 00:44:24.429
I love my work.

818
00:44:24.429 --> 00:44:26.317
I love the people I work with.

819
00:44:26.317 --> 00:44:27.667
<v ARSENEAULT>And what’s your wife’s name?

820
00:44:27.667 --> 00:44:27.937
<v MCMILLIN>Sue.

821
00:44:27.937 --> 00:44:28.206
Sweetheart.

822
00:44:28.206 --> 00:44:28.746
Second wife.

823
00:44:28.746 --> 00:44:30.365
Been married . . . be twenty-six years July

824
00:44:30.365 --> 00:44:30.635
<c.p>twentieth.

825
00:44:30.635 --> 00:44:31.984
<v ARSENEAULT>And where did y’all meet?

826
00:44:31.984 --> 00:44:32.524
<v MCMILLIN>High school.

827
00:44:32.524 --> 00:44:34.683
She wouldn’t have nothing to do with me.

828
00:44:34.683 --> 00:44:37.111
We got back together our fifteen years class reunion.

829
00:44:37.111 --> 00:44:39.000
That’s a whole ‘nother story, but . . .

830
00:44:39.000 --> 00:44:39.315
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

831
00:44:39.315 --> 00:44:42.782
<v MCMILLIN>She wouldn’t have much to do with me in high school.

832
00:44:42.782 --> 00:44:45.303
She said I was too fun loving [laughs].

833
00:44:45.303 --> 00:44:46.564
<v ARSENEAULT>Too many fun coupons.

834
00:44:46.564 --> 00:44:52.552
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah I was having a lot of fun and she was like, “That guy’s a little wild for me.”

835
00:44:52.552 --> 00:44:52.867
<v ARSENEAULT>Trouble.

836
00:44:52.867 --> 00:44:59.170
<v MCMILLIN>“He’s . . .” But then at our fifteen year class reunion I was single and she had gotten back with her ex.

837
00:44:59.170 --> 00:44:59.800
We danced.

838
00:44:59.800 --> 00:45:03.582
I always liked her and we had kind of had a connection.

839
00:45:03.582 --> 00:45:13.982
And then three months later I was going to a nightclub and I happened to stop at another night club called Charlie’s before I was there and she was at a retirement party.

840
00:45:13.982 --> 00:45:15.242
And I’m like, “Hey

841
00:45:15.242 --> 00:45:18.394
<c.p>where’s your ex?” and she’s like, “Well we split up.”

842
00:45:18.394 --> 00:45:20.915
And I’m like, get that scope on there.

843
00:45:20.915 --> 00:45:26.273
And she’s already telling her girlfriend, “Hey don’t leave me this guy’s going to be following me.”

844
00:45:26.273 --> 00:45:27.848
And I started stalking her.

845
00:45:27.848 --> 00:45:28.794
I really did.

846
00:45:28.794 --> 00:45:31.000
I had to hunt her down.

847
00:45:31.000 --> 00:45:36.241
Knew her mom and I called her mom, and her mom kept saying, “No, she’s not here.”

848
00:45:36.241 --> 00:45:42.099
But I knew she was living with her mom because her brother told me that so I’d keep calling.

849
00:45:42.099 --> 00:45:45.490
And finally she says, one time her mom . . . She liked me.

850
00:45:45.490 --> 00:45:49.806
I knew her mom from a business that her mom used to be in.

851
00:45:49.806 --> 00:45:54.431
Mom said, “Sue you just going to have to talk to this little McMillin boy.

852
00:45:54.431 --> 00:45:56.897
I just can’t lie to him no more.”

853
00:45:56.897 --> 00:46:04.296
So she took the phone and I finally got a date with her and she realized that I wasn’t the same guy in 1973.

854
00:46:04.296 --> 00:46:10.462
Although I had a few of the traits, but when you find your soulmate, and you find your best friend,

855
00:46:10.462 --> 00:46:18.787
and you marry her . . . We lived together a little while before we got married because we both didn’t trust . . . both going through divorces and stuff like that.

856
00:46:18.787 --> 00:46:23.719
And she had two kids, Patrick and Amy, and I had two kids, Marsha and Boon.

857
00:46:23.719 --> 00:46:24.644
But it worked.

858
00:46:24.644 --> 00:46:25.261
It worked.

859
00:46:25.261 --> 00:46:32.968
And that’s been probably one of the greatest things in our life is that I’ve had the opportunity to be married to my best friend.

860
00:46:32.968 --> 00:46:37.901
And the greatest thing about Sue is that she lets me be Hal all the time.

861
00:46:37.901 --> 00:46:42.834
I can be the same Hal sitting here messing with you, cutting up, she knows me.

862
00:46:42.834 --> 00:46:44.067
She knows my personality.

863
00:46:44.067 --> 00:46:45.609
She knows who I am.

864
00:46:45.609 --> 00:46:46.842
I’m just so blessed.

865
00:46:46.842 --> 00:46:47.767
And she’s pretty.

866
00:46:47.767 --> 00:46:49.000
She’s real pretty.

867
00:46:49.000 --> 00:46:50.125
<v ARSENEAULT>That doesn’t hurt.

868
00:46:50.125 --> 00:46:53.500
<v MCMILLIN>All my buddies say I outran the coverage [laughs].

869
00:46:53.500 --> 00:46:55.000
Way above my head.

870
00:46:55.000 --> 00:46:56.125
She’s a sweetheart.

871
00:46:56.125 --> 00:46:58.000
She’s a good girl.

872
00:46:58.000 --> 00:47:00.179
<v ARSENEAULT>So I have to ask.

873
00:47:00.179 --> 00:47:04.974
So you are pro-industry, but you’re also it sounds like pro-environment.

874
00:47:04.974 --> 00:47:07.590
You want conservation and you want . . .

875
00:47:07.590 --> 00:47:08.026
<v MCMILLIN>[Agrees].

876
00:47:08.026 --> 00:47:08.462
Yeah.

877
00:47:08.462 --> 00:47:12.385
<v ARSENEAULT>How do you reconcile that when it sometimes doesn’t . . .?

878
00:47:12.385 --> 00:47:13.692
<v MCMILLIN>Well, it does.

879
00:47:13.692 --> 00:47:15.000
It really does.

880
00:47:15.000 --> 00:47:19.359
I mean we have the agencies that watch the industries.

881
00:47:19.359 --> 00:47:30.692
I was part of the environmental group for five years, so I have first-hand knowledge of going on inspections with the DEQ [Department of Environmental Quality].

882
00:47:30.692 --> 00:47:42.026
I have first-hand knowledge of the EPA [Environmental Protection Agency] being in the plant, and those guys take their job very serious and they watch out.

883
00:47:42.026 --> 00:47:44.205
And that was in 1999.

884
00:47:44.205 --> 00:47:51.179
The industries like everything else out there, whether it’s a computer or any . . . from the iPhones,

885
00:47:51.179 --> 00:47:55.974
have exponentially grown greatly in their ability to be more efficient.

886
00:47:55.974 --> 00:48:00.333
Well that was a big fly in there, wasn’t it?

887
00:48:00.333 --> 00:48:06.000
Exponentially gotten better to grow in their abilities to do things better.

888
00:48:06.000 --> 00:48:10.391
The industries, they don’t . . . They try not to have any upsets,

889
00:48:10.391 --> 00:48:16.777
and try to not to hurt our environment, and they have checks and balances in there.

890
00:48:16.777 --> 00:48:24.361
I was a part of the era that in the 70s wasn’t near as restricted as it is now.

891
00:48:24.361 --> 00:48:27.155
But we wasn’t ever hurting the environment.

892
00:48:27.155 --> 00:48:30.748
We . . . but they’re very strict and they watch it.

893
00:48:30.748 --> 00:48:35.538
And they’re very . . . we’ve got really good agencies that watch our environment.

894
00:48:35.538 --> 00:48:40.328
So I have a lot of faith in what those guys doing.

895
00:48:40.328 --> 00:48:52.702
I mean I don’t feel like our plants are here . . . Is there a bit of a trade off with the lights and the noise and the traffic and things like this?

896
00:48:52.702 --> 00:48:53.101
Absolutely.

897
00:48:53.101 --> 00:48:56.294
But look at the good jobs we have.

898
00:48:56.294 --> 00:48:59.487
Look at . . . As I talked earlier about the

899
00:48:59.487 --> 00:49:05.874
<c.p>401Ks, and insurance, and the great lives you can have, and the vacations you can take,

900
00:49:05.874 --> 00:49:14.655
and the homecoming gowns you can buy for your kids, and afford things because you have a good job in the industry.

901
00:49:14.655 --> 00:49:15.454
So, yeah.

902
00:49:15.454 --> 00:49:17.450
And I am an outdoorsman.

903
00:49:17.450 --> 00:49:19.845
And they put a lot back.

904
00:49:19.845 --> 00:49:23.038
They put a lot back to the outdoors.

905
00:49:23.038 --> 00:49:27.828
Or as an auctioneer I often see whether it’s the industry itself,

906
00:49:27.828 --> 00:49:37.008
or a contractor of the industry spending money with Delta Waterfowl, Ducks Unlimited, Whitetail deer, the different auctions that I get to do.

907
00:49:37.008 --> 00:49:41.000
They put a lot back into the community and . . .

908
00:49:41.000 --> 00:49:42.429
<v ARSENEAULT>How about any accidental spills?

909
00:49:42.429 --> 00:49:47.000
Like I know . . . I don’t know if it was Conoco, but Condea Vista in the . . . Mossville.

910
00:49:47.000 --> 00:49:48.714
I don’t know if you remember.

911
00:49:48.714 --> 00:49:50.429
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah the EDC [ethylene dichloride] spill?

912
00:49:50.429 --> 00:49:51.000
<v ARSENEAULT>[Agrees].

913
00:49:51.000 --> 00:49:51.371
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah.

914
00:49:51.371 --> 00:49:52.482
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember?

915
00:49:52.482 --> 00:49:55.817
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah I was right in the middle of it.

916
00:49:55.817 --> 00:49:57.670
I know all about it.

917
00:49:57.670 --> 00:50:01.004
And I know that there’s been remediation of that.

918
00:50:01.004 --> 00:50:04.710
There was a . . . They’d put all kind of wells in.

919
00:50:04.710 --> 00:50:08.415
That we used to check the sands in those wells.

920
00:50:08.415 --> 00:50:14.714
Check to see if there was any contamination and then different levels whether it was twenty foot,

921
00:50:14.714 --> 00:50:18.420
eighty foot, hundred foot . . . but there has been mistakes made.

922
00:50:18.420 --> 00:50:24.348
I mean my gosh, we’re all capable of not doing a hundred percent all the time.

923
00:50:24.348 --> 00:50:25.089
But they’ve

924
00:50:25.089 --> 00:50:28.424
<c.p>done a lot of work to remediate all that.

925
00:50:28.424 --> 00:50:35.094
And they bought out a lot of people in that Bel Air area that they thought was affected.

926
00:50:35.094 --> 00:50:39.911
So they’ve done the right thing in my mind to make things better.

927
00:50:39.911 --> 00:50:45.469
And anytime you can have what essentially started out as a nine billion dollar project,

928
00:50:45.469 --> 00:50:51.397
and now eleven million dollar project, right here in my police jury district, in my hometown,

929
00:50:51.397 --> 00:51:02.513
that hopefully my kids and grandkids will have opportunity to work at and stay here in southwest Louisiana and not have to move off because they don’t have opportunities here.

930
00:51:02.513 --> 00:51:10.665
And that’s one thing that I guess as an elected official working with these industries is give the local people first shot.

931
00:51:10.665 --> 00:51:14.000
And they have been pretty good about that.

932
00:51:14.000 --> 00:51:16.698
<v ARSENEAULT>What inspired you to become a councilman after . . .?

933
00:51:16.698 --> 00:51:17.709
<v MCMILLIN>Oh, my mom.

934
00:51:17.709 --> 00:51:18.047
Yeah.

935
00:51:18.047 --> 00:51:25.802
Growing up through the whole era of watching my mom and going to these conventions and seeing Governor Edwards at an early age,

936
00:51:25.802 --> 00:51:32.209
or governor John McKeithen . . . seeing all these people. [It made me say?], “Hey I want to do that someday.”

937
00:51:32.209 --> 00:51:41.988
But this is the way I started out and believe it or not I used to be very very nervous when I’d get in front of people to speak.

938
00:51:41.988 --> 00:51:42.663
Had butterflies.

939
00:51:42.663 --> 00:51:45.360
And then I thought, “What is this feeling?

940
00:51:45.360 --> 00:51:46.372
Man, I’m nervous.”

941
00:51:46.372 --> 00:51:52.779
But if you take that feeling . . . You can’t get that feeling from any drink, or any drug, or anything.

942
00:51:52.779 --> 00:51:56.488
That’s . . . That feeling, that fear of speaking in front of people,

943
00:51:56.488 --> 00:52:01.547
what if it’s a good feeling and I’m just not realizing it’s a good feeling?

944
00:52:01.547 --> 00:52:05.256
And I started thinking, okay I want to feel this feeling.

945
00:52:05.256 --> 00:52:09.302
I want to feel what it feels like to get this rush.

946
00:52:09.302 --> 00:52:10.651
This is not negative.

947
00:52:10.651 --> 00:52:12.000
Feel that energy.

948
00:52:12.000 --> 00:52:19.352
So I started off as PTO president, and I was PTO president when my kids were in school.

949
00:52:19.352 --> 00:52:24.662
Then I was on the Westlake . . . Mossville and Westlake recreation district where I

950
00:52:24.662 --> 00:52:30.380
<c.p>worked with Mossville and Westlake, on both, as a commissioner for the recreation district.

951
00:52:30.380 --> 00:52:32.831
Then I ran for political office.

952
00:52:32.831 --> 00:52:33.648
Got beat.

953
00:52:33.648 --> 00:52:36.099
First time I ran I lost.

954
00:52:36.099 --> 00:52:37.732
Like to kill me.

955
00:52:37.732 --> 00:52:41.000
I think, ‘Oh my gosh what happened?’

956
00:52:41.000 --> 00:52:42.961
<v ARSENEAULT>What’s the election process like?

957
00:52:42.961 --> 00:52:44.137
Did you campaign?

958
00:52:44.137 --> 00:52:48.059
<v MCMILLIN>I campaigned, but I was new and I was young.

959
00:52:48.059 --> 00:52:55.510
Look, I was that hometown guy that may have been known as the party guy, the fun loving guy.

960
00:52:55.510 --> 00:52:57.471
Can this guy be serious?

961
00:52:57.471 --> 00:52:59.431
I had to overcome that.

962
00:52:59.431 --> 00:53:07.667
I had to go back and work harder at different events to make sure people saw me in a different light.

963
00:53:07.667 --> 00:53:10.804
That I wasn’t just some class clown, okay?

964
00:53:10.804 --> 00:53:13.549
Because you’re always seventeen in your hometown.

965
00:53:13.549 --> 00:53:22.569
And some of the things that you did . . . I’ll never forget the first time I knocked on Ms. Trahan’s door and I said,

966
00:53:22.569 --> 00:53:29.235
“Ms. Trahan, I’m Hal McMillin I’m running for city council and I’d really like to . . . Your support.

967
00:53:29.235 --> 00:53:33.941
I think I can get in there and do a good job.”

968
00:53:33.941 --> 00:53:37.471
She looked at me and she said, “Hal McMillin.

969
00:53:37.471 --> 00:53:41.784
Aren’t you that little boy that threw tomatoes at my car?”

970
00:53:41.784 --> 00:53:46.098
I said, “Yes ma’am, but I’ve outgrown that stage.” [Both laugh].

971
00:53:46.098 --> 00:53:50.020
So there’s things like that that you got to overcome.

972
00:53:50.020 --> 00:53:57.471
You got to go back and you’ve got to build confidence in Ms. Trahan that she’ll vote for you.

973
00:53:57.471 --> 00:54:01.784
So I got beat the first time, but I worked harder.

974
00:54:01.784 --> 00:54:04.529
I didn’t let defeat be a negative.

975
00:54:04.529 --> 00:54:06.882
I took it as a positive.

976
00:54:06.882 --> 00:54:07.667
Said okay.

977
00:54:07.667 --> 00:54:10.804
So I worked harder on the recreation board.

978
00:54:10.804 --> 00:54:13.549
I got involved in the Kiwanis Club.

979
00:54:13.549 --> 00:54:18.647
I did more things for the community to show, hey I can lead.

980
00:54:18.647 --> 00:54:28.451
I got elected the second time to the city council and served on there one term, and then ran and got elected my second term.

981
00:54:28.451 --> 00:54:36.294
Then the opportunity to run for police juror came up and I ran for police juror and won that election.

982
00:54:36.294 --> 00:54:41.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And that includes like, council for like the Mossville area, too?

983
00:54:41.000 --> 00:54:41.220
<v MCMILLIN>Well police juror does.

984
00:54:41.220 --> 00:54:41.495
Police juror is Westlake, Mossville.

985
00:54:41.495 --> 00:54:42.593
Mossville was about half in my district when I first got in there, but now it’s entirely in my district.

986
00:54:42.593 --> 00:54:42.813
<v ARSENEAULT>And it’s still wards?

987
00:54:42.813 --> 00:54:43.013
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah.

988
00:54:42.868 --> 00:54:43.857
Ward three . . . Ward four is the . . . I have some in ward four and ward six in my ward,

989
00:54:43.857 --> 00:54:44.187
but the boundary lines have changed.

990
00:54:44.187 --> 00:54:45.396
Usually half of Westlake was on Ms. Griffin’s side of Lake Charles, but I have all of Westlake and all of Mossville.

991
00:54:45.396 --> 00:54:46.000
And it’s a [compass?] in my police jury district, so.

992
00:54:46.000 --> 00:54:47.739
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about Mossville?

993
00:54:47.739 --> 00:54:49.768
Like your first impressions of the area?

994
00:54:49.768 --> 00:54:51.507
<v MCMILLIN>I’ve known Mossville all my life.

995
00:54:51.507 --> 00:54:52.087
<v ARSENEAULT>So you . . .

996
00:54:52.087 --> 00:54:57.594
<v MCMILLIN>I mean my dad was a hometown plumber so we had . . . He had a lot of friends over there.

997
00:54:57.594 --> 00:55:01.942
They . . . whether it was from horses, or beagles . . . A lot of blacks enjoy rabbit hunting.

998
00:55:01.942 --> 00:55:03.391
And a lot of blacks . . .

999
00:55:03.391 --> 00:55:04.841
<v ARSENEAULT>And beagles are rabbit dogs?

1000
00:55:04.841 --> 00:55:06.000
<v MCMILLIN>Rabbit dogs, yeah.

1001
00:55:06.000 --> 00:55:06.367
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1002
00:55:06.367 --> 00:55:07.469
<v MCMILLIN>They’re rabbit dogs.

1003
00:55:07.469 --> 00:55:12.245
And we would . . . My dad would go over and see people from Mossville.

1004
00:55:12.245 --> 00:55:15.184
Here’s something I remember at an early age.

1005
00:55:15.184 --> 00:55:20.694
Mossville High School had a very good football team and they would play in Westlake,

1006
00:55:20.694 --> 00:55:24.735
but they didn’t really have any . . . They had a few stands.

1007
00:55:24.735 --> 00:55:33.184
They didn’t have much, but we would go and sit in the car and watch the Mossville football team who was really good.

1008
00:55:33.184 --> 00:55:36.490
In the mid-60s they had some really good athletes.

1009
00:55:36.490 --> 00:55:39.796
In fact, even some of them went to pros.

1010
00:55:39.796 --> 00:55:42.000
But . . . so I remember that.

1011
00:55:42.000 --> 00:55:42.740
<v ARSENEAULT>Richard Lee.

1012
00:55:42.740 --> 00:55:43.480
<v MCMILLIN>Richard Lee.

1013
00:55:43.480 --> 00:55:43.850
Yeah.

1014
00:55:43.850 --> 00:55:45.701
Richard . . . Good friend of mine.

1015
00:55:45.701 --> 00:55:48.291
I worked with him at the plant.

1016
00:55:48.291 --> 00:55:49.402
A good guy.

1017
00:55:49.402 --> 00:55:50.142
Joe Comeaux.

1018
00:55:50.142 --> 00:55:54.953
Joe Comeaux never went to the pros, but had played for Grambling [University].

1019
00:55:54.953 --> 00:55:57.543
The whole Comeaux family was very good.

1020
00:55:57.543 --> 00:56:01.984
So a lot of really good football players and stuff like this.

1021
00:56:01.984 --> 00:56:05.685
And then I had a lot of friends in Mossville.

1022
00:56:05.685 --> 00:56:08.646
I mean its . . . it just . . . The Dellafosse family,

1023
00:56:08.646 --> 00:56:12.717
the Joe [Bear?] family, the Rigmaiden family . . . They’re just a lot.

1024
00:56:12.717 --> 00:56:19.748
I [got a feeling?] I’m going to leave some out and I really shouldn’t, but I made friends easy.

1025
00:56:19.748 --> 00:56:28.630
I didn’t look at color of skin or . . . I looked at people’s hearts and if they were good people then we became good friends.

1026
00:56:28.630 --> 00:56:29.740
That was fun.

1027
00:56:29.740 --> 00:56:36.772
<c.p>I guess really . . . another involvement with Mossville was through Mr. Rigmaiden, Josh Rigmaiden, who the center is named after.

1028
00:56:36.772 --> 00:56:42.323
He was on the recreation board with me and we did a lot of things.

1029
00:56:42.323 --> 00:56:47.134
We couldn’t agree on everything, but we did a lot of things together.

1030
00:56:47.134 --> 00:56:50.465
Usually when we’d do something in the Westlake area,

1031
00:56:50.465 --> 00:56:58.236
we’d make sure we’d do something similar in the Mossville area because Mossville had a big base of kids out there.

1032
00:56:58.236 --> 00:57:04.157
So we . . . The recreation district brought Westlake and Mossville together in a lot of ways, so.

1033
00:57:04.157 --> 00:57:06.748
I always loved going out there, too.

1034
00:57:06.748 --> 00:57:16.000
Anytime I smelled barbeque smoke coming out of that recreation center out there I usually try to stop by and see who I knew.

1035
00:57:16.000 --> 00:57:22.380
Coach Williams, a true mentor to me, high school teacher, one of the greatest men I’ve ever known.

1036
00:57:22.380 --> 00:57:23.443
Never saw color.

1037
00:57:23.443 --> 00:57:27.696
He was always a peaceful guy and very good friend of mine.

1038
00:57:27.696 --> 00:57:32.304
He’s one of the people who’s played an important role in my life.

1039
00:57:32.304 --> 00:57:36.203
So we had a lot of good people from Mossville area.

1040
00:57:36.203 --> 00:57:41.165
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you ever serve on the water board or anything like that in Mossville?

1041
00:57:41.165 --> 00:57:42.228
<v MCMILLIN>No, I didn’t.

1042
00:57:42.228 --> 00:57:43.291
<v ARSENEAULT>Was that all . . .?

1043
00:57:43.291 --> 00:57:44.000
Okay.

1044
00:57:44.000 --> 00:57:46.844
<v MCMILLIN>No I didn’t serve on a water board,

1045
00:57:46.844 --> 00:57:56.089
but as police juror I appointed people to the water board and I had a lot of really good people on that water district out there.

1046
00:57:56.089 --> 00:58:01.067
Blunt . . . There’s a number of people that on the different water boards and districts.

1047
00:58:01.067 --> 00:58:04.978
Raymond [Malbow?], great guy, I went to high school with Raymond.

1048
00:58:04.978 --> 00:58:06.400
Raymond was one of

1049
00:58:06.400 --> 00:58:09.244
<c.p>those guys that applied himself if high school.

1050
00:58:09.244 --> 00:58:12.444
Really smart guy and a good friend today, so.

1051
00:58:12.444 --> 00:58:13.156
Good folks.

1052
00:58:13.156 --> 00:58:14.933
<v ARSENEAULT>When did Mossville get water?

1053
00:58:14.933 --> 00:58:16.000
I’m trying . . .

1054
00:58:16.000 --> 00:58:16.375
<v MCMILLIN>I don’t know the exact day.

1055
00:58:16.375 --> 00:58:16.575
<v ARSENEAULT>Exact day.

1056
00:58:16.500 --> 00:58:17.000
<v MCMILLIN>It had it before I got there.

1057
00:58:17.000 --> 00:58:17.292
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1058
00:58:17.292 --> 00:58:20.215
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah they had a water district so they had it.

1059
00:58:20.215 --> 00:58:23.431
Some other police jurors prior to me did that water district,

1060
00:58:23.431 --> 00:58:30.154
but yeah I’ve always felt like Mossville . . . From my police jury standpoint having a lot of friends out there, always fought for Mossville.

1061
00:58:30.154 --> 00:58:34.538
Whatever they would need I would try to be the person they would . . . go-to guy.

1062
00:58:34.538 --> 00:58:41.554
That’s one thing I learned very early in politics, is if you’re going to be in politics and you’re going to be a servant,

1063
00:58:41.554 --> 00:58:45.062
you need to serve and you need to serve all the people.

1064
00:58:45.062 --> 00:58:49.738
And once you get elected it doesn’t matter if that person voted for you, or not.

1065
00:58:49.738 --> 00:58:52.369
They’re your constituent and you need to serve them.

1066
00:58:52.369 --> 00:58:56.462
So I always liked being the conduit between the problem and solving the problem.

1067
00:58:56.462 --> 00:59:02.308
And that’s what I talked about earlier, is I’ve learned through relationships that I don’t have to do it all.

1068
00:59:02.308 --> 00:59:07.277
I’ve got people, really good people in the parish that I can assign stuff to and it

1069
00:59:07.277 --> 00:59:07.862
<c.p>gets done.

1070
00:59:07.862 --> 00:59:11.662
Now, one thing they know about me is I always close the circle.

1071
00:59:11.662 --> 00:59:16.338
If I talk to somebody and they have a problem and I assign this to them,

1072
00:59:16.338 --> 00:59:21.015
then I’m going to come back and talk to them and make sure it got did.

1073
00:59:21.015 --> 00:59:26.569
And I’m going to come . . . Go back to that person and make sure they were satisfied with the effort.

1074
00:59:26.569 --> 00:59:33.292
So that’s just part of the political stuff that you learn to do and I think it’s the right way to do it.

1075
00:59:33.292 --> 00:59:40.308
I had one time in a meeting a guy say, “Well I’ve been trying to call you I couldn’t get in touch with you.”

1076
00:59:40.308 --> 00:59:42.062
That’s my number one pet peeve.

1077
00:59:42.062 --> 00:59:44.108
You can get in touch with me.

1078
00:59:44.108 --> 00:59:48.492
My cell phone number is one of the easiest ones in the parish: 4-8-8 4-8-4-8.

1079
00:59:48.492 --> 00:59:52.000
If you can’t get in touch with me, shame on you.

1080
00:59:52.000 --> 00:59:53.807
<v ARSENEAULT>You should be an injury lawyer.

1081
00:59:53.807 --> 00:59:54.711
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah I should.

1082
00:59:54.711 --> 00:59:55.614
It’s right here.

1083
00:59:55.614 --> 00:59:58.928
It’s an iPhone on my hip and I check it often.

1084
00:59:58.928 --> 01:00:01.337
I call people back that butt dial me.

1085
01:00:01.337 --> 01:00:04.349
They’re like, “Oh man I just butt dialed you, sorry.”

1086
01:00:04.349 --> 01:00:09.169
I said, “Well I saw you called I wanted to make sure you didn’t need something.”

1087
01:00:09.169 --> 01:00:11.578
But that’s part of being a public servant.

1088
01:00:11.578 --> 01:00:13.386
So it’s been a good run.

1089
01:00:13.386 --> 01:00:15.795
<v ARSENEAULT>So you were a councilman from ’99 . . . ’95 . . .

1090
01:00:15.795 --> 01:00:17.000
<v MCMILLIN>Five to ’99 . . .

1091
01:00:17.000 --> 01:00:17.500
<v ARSENEAULT>To ’99.

1092
01:00:17.500 --> 01:00:18.000
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah.

1093
01:00:18.000 --> 01:00:20.700
<v ARSENEAULT>And then you decided to run for police . . .?

1094
01:00:20.700 --> 01:00:21.037
<v MCMILLIN>Yes.

1095
01:00:21.037 --> 01:00:22.049
<v ARSENEAULT>What made you . . .?

1096
01:00:22.049 --> 01:00:27.786
<v MCMILLIN>Well I had some people that . . . The police jurors in there who was a friend of mine.

1097
01:00:27.786 --> 01:00:29.811
That was a pretty tough run.

1098
01:00:29.811 --> 01:00:31.498
A guy named David Abshire.

1099
01:00:31.498 --> 01:00:38.247
They felt like they wanted someone different and they came to me and talked to me about it and said,

1100
01:00:38.247 --> 01:00:40.609
“Hey we feel like you’re the guy.

1101
01:00:40.609 --> 01:00:44.658
That it’s your turn to run for this and we’ll help you.”

1102
01:00:44.658 --> 01:00:53.432
And I guess really it kind of makes you feel pretty good when a group of people come to you and say, “Hey we want you.”

1103
01:00:53.432 --> 01:00:55.119
Wow that’s a good feeling.

1104
01:00:55.119 --> 01:00:57.144
And, “We’re going to support you.”

1105
01:00:57.144 --> 01:01:00.519
Now, I didn’t think I was going to win [laughs].

1106
01:01:00.519 --> 01:01:03.218
I didn’t think I was going to win.

1107
01:01:03.218 --> 01:01:06.930
I thought, “Wow this guy’s an incumbent police juror,” and stuff.

1108
01:01:06.930 --> 01:01:16.716
But I’d started doing the outdoor tips on KPLC and I was getting a lot of exposure on T.V. from . . . And once a week I do an outdoor tip.

1109
01:01:16.716 --> 01:01:19.078
This was before I did it monthly.

1110
01:01:19.078 --> 01:01:21.440
I was doing it once a week.

1111
01:01:21.440 --> 01:01:22.790
And people knew me.

1112
01:01:22.790 --> 01:01:30.214
And I guess they saw that I was sincere in wanting to be a police juror and they gave me an opportunity.

1113
01:01:30.214 --> 01:01:31.901
And I won fifty-two, forty-eight.

1114
01:01:31.901 --> 01:01:32.576
Close election.

1115
01:01:32.576 --> 01:01:40.000
It surprised not only me, but a lot of people, but I won and I’ve loved being on there ever since.

1116
01:01:40.000 --> 01:01:41.574
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it a cordial . . .?

1117
01:01:41.574 --> 01:01:43.542
<v MCMILLIN>No. No, it wasn’t cordial.

1118
01:01:43.542 --> 01:01:44.329
It wasn’t.

1119
01:01:44.329 --> 01:01:48.264
No, he was hurt and I can see how he

1120
01:01:48.264 --> 01:01:54.954
<c.p>would be hurt because when someone runs against me now, it would hurt if they beat me.

1121
01:01:54.954 --> 01:01:58.495
So we . . . it took time for that to heal.

1122
01:01:58.495 --> 01:02:00.069
He’s a great man,

1123
01:02:00.069 --> 01:02:08.333
but I just . . . I seized the opportunity and the people gave me that opportunity and it’s hard to run against someone.

1124
01:02:08.333 --> 01:02:08.727
Yeah.

1125
01:02:08.727 --> 01:02:09.514
It is.

1126
01:02:09.514 --> 01:02:11.088
And it hurt him.

1127
01:02:11.088 --> 01:02:12.662
I say it did.

1128
01:02:12.662 --> 01:02:17.778
He was mad at me for a while, but that’s healed up now.

1129
01:02:17.778 --> 01:02:23.287
So that’s not . . . Sometimes there’s some things in politics that are not real fun.

1130
01:02:23.287 --> 01:02:24.074
It’s tough.

1131
01:02:24.074 --> 01:02:27.222
The toughest decision in politics is zoning issues.

1132
01:02:27.222 --> 01:02:34.699
Any time you have a zoning issue you’ve got a whole group of people over here that want it,

1133
01:02:34.699 --> 01:02:39.421
and a whole group of people over here that don’t want it.

1134
01:02:39.421 --> 01:02:42.963
I kin it to being like a basketball official.

1135
01:02:42.963 --> 01:02:52.801
When a basketball official blows a whistle, usually fifty percent this team’s not going to like it, and this team is, and that team’s not.

1136
01:02:52.801 --> 01:02:59.491
So it’s almost like whenever you’re doing a zoning case, you’re going to have people at odds.

1137
01:02:59.491 --> 01:03:03.819
Those are the toughest and I take them the most serious.

1138
01:03:03.819 --> 01:03:05.000
But yeah.

1139
01:03:05.000 --> 01:03:07.890
<v ARSENEAULT>What about rezoning Mossville into heavy industrial?

1140
01:03:07.890 --> 01:03:08.302
<v MCMILLIN>Tough.

1141
01:03:08.302 --> 01:03:08.715
Tough.

1142
01:03:08.715 --> 01:03:10.366
<v ARSENEAULT>What was that like?

1143
01:03:10.366 --> 01:03:12.017
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah that’s tough decisions.

1144
01:03:12.017 --> 01:03:22.337
You see the factors of what Sasol’s going to bring with this economic development, but you also see people that’s lived there all their life.

1145
01:03:22.337 --> 01:03:26.052
But in my opinion, Sasol was more than fair.

1146
01:03:26.052 --> 01:03:31.831
The people that they . . . needed their property for a project was paid very well.

1147
01:03:31.831 --> 01:03:37.198
The people that they just wanted to be a good neighbor and say,

1148
01:03:37.198 --> 01:03:44.628
“Hey we’re going to give you an opportunity to sell on the VPP [voluntary property purchase] plan and

1149
01:03:44.628 --> 01:03:47.517
<c.p>buy out sixty percent above appraised value.”

1150
01:03:47.517 --> 01:03:53.297
Plus paying closing costs, plus incentives if you signed early and stuff like this.

1151
01:03:53.297 --> 01:04:00.727
You offer me sixty percent above appraised value of my house right now, you can have the close.

1152
01:04:00.727 --> 01:04:01.965
I’ll just go.

1153
01:04:01.965 --> 01:04:03.616
That’s a tremendous offer.

1154
01:04:03.616 --> 01:04:10.221
In fact, I think it’s even better than . . . We all remember the history of Love Canal.

1155
01:04:10.221 --> 01:04:16.000
That was a better offer than what the people got at Love Canal.

1156
01:04:16.000 --> 01:04:19.812
So I feel like Sasol’s been very fair in their offer.

1157
01:04:19.812 --> 01:04:27.435
I feel like there are some people in Mossville had been very unfair in wanting to stay and try to get more.

1158
01:04:27.435 --> 01:04:29.167
And that’s a tough situation.

1159
01:04:29.167 --> 01:04:35.405
Some people want to stay because they want to stay . . . They don’t want to leave their home site.

1160
01:04:35.405 --> 01:04:39.909
But there are some people that want to stay because they’re holding out.

1161
01:04:39.909 --> 01:04:44.414
And I think those people are going to lose in the long run.

1162
01:04:44.414 --> 01:04:48.226
They should have took the very fair offer from the beginning.

1163
01:04:48.226 --> 01:04:51.344
My opinion, but you can point-counterpoint that all day.

1164
01:04:51.344 --> 01:04:58.621
But my opinion is Sasol as an industry with they’ve done has been very fair to the majority of the people.

1165
01:04:58.621 --> 01:05:05.898
There’s some people that didn’t want to leave, and it hurts them to leave, and it’s their community and their history,

1166
01:05:05.898 --> 01:05:14.560
but for the better good of the entire Calcasieu Parish and everything, we can’t let an eleven billion dollar project not come to southwest Louisiana.

1167
01:05:14.560 --> 01:05:15.947
Is there growing pains?

1168
01:05:15.947 --> 01:05:16.293
Absolutely.

1169
01:05:16.293 --> 01:05:21.837
Is there people that having to leave their home that didn’t want to leave their home?

1170
01:05:21.837 --> 01:05:22.184
Yes.

1171
01:05:22.184 --> 01:05:27.728
Is there people that are waiting in traffic much longer or at the restaurant much longer?

1172
01:05:27.728 --> 01:05:28.767
We all are.

1173
01:05:28.767 --> 01:05:35.351
But it’s going to be better in the long run for all of us and I’m excited about that.

1174
01:05:35.351 --> 01:05:36.391
I really am.

1175
01:05:36.391 --> 01:05:46.093
I think that the legacy of what we’re trying to do right now as good elected officials is leave a legacy from thirty to forty years from now.

1176
01:05:46.093 --> 01:05:53.023
They’ll look back and say wow, these folks have . . . they really were doing the right thing in some tough situations.

1177
01:05:53.023 --> 01:06:01.686
One of the things that we’ve done and I’ve been a core group member of this from day one and was a chairmen of this

1178
01:06:01.686 --> 01:06:08.616
<c.p>now that we’ve hired a director and facilitor, but I’ve been in the go group since it started, since inception.

1179
01:06:08.616 --> 01:06:23.863
Since the first mill at the pioneer club saying hey we need to be proactive in southwest Louisiana to come up with what do we need to do to structure the growth that thirty to forty years from now people will go back say,

1180
01:06:23.863 --> 01:06:25.595
“Wow, those guys weren’t thinking.”

1181
01:06:25.595 --> 01:06:31.140
We want to try to be proactive and the industries are working with us on that.

1182
01:06:31.140 --> 01:06:32.179
They really are.

1183
01:06:32.179 --> 01:06:40.149
I mean Mike Hayes and Kim Cusumano, I got both of them cell numbers and we talk often and we don’t always agree,

1184
01:06:40.149 --> 01:06:45.000
but we can agree to disagree and still be friends on different things.

1185
01:06:45.000 --> 01:06:49.624
<v ARSENEAULT>So police jury, your duties are zoning, what are some other?

1186
01:06:49.624 --> 01:06:52.566
<v MCMILLIN>Well, we do a lot of things.

1187
01:06:52.566 --> 01:06:58.451
We do all the infrastructure stuff on parish roads, drainage is huge . . . That’s us.

1188
01:06:58.451 --> 01:07:04.756
Of course we have signage, drainage, infrastructure from . . . we have water districts, some sewer districts,

1189
01:07:04.756 --> 01:07:15.264
we take care of other things like the jailhouse, and then the courthouse, we take care of mosquito control, we take care of the libraries.

1190
01:07:15.264 --> 01:07:19.047
Being a police jury encompasses a lot of things.

1191
01:07:19.047 --> 01:07:20.729
A lot of things.

1192
01:07:20.729 --> 01:07:31.658
But really it’s a day to day individual constituent that may have a problem that his trash can’s busted, or his trash didn’t get picked up.

1193
01:07:31.658 --> 01:07:38.803
Or my ditch is . . . The grass is high and my ditch and my ditch ain’t draining right,

1194
01:07:38.803 --> 01:07:45.108
or hey my neighbor’s septic system isn’t working and I need somebody to come out.

1195
01:07:45.108 --> 01:07:46.790
All kinds of calls.

1196
01:07:46.790 --> 01:07:48.892
I get tons of calls.

1197
01:07:48.892 --> 01:07:56.458
Tree limb fell on this property, or on this line, or there’s . . . That’s what I enjoy about it.

1198
01:07:56.458 --> 01:08:01.502
Every day is . . . brings a different set of opportunities to help somebody.

1199
01:08:01.502 --> 01:08:04.444
<v ARSENEAULT>What’s the craziest call you can remember?

1200
01:08:04.444 --> 01:08:05.705
<v MCMILLIN>It wasn’t me.

1201
01:08:05.705 --> 01:08:08.227
It was another buddy of mine.

1202
01:08:08.227 --> 01:08:12.851
He had a call from a constituent that had this duck.

1203
01:08:12.851 --> 01:08:27.983
That the duck was attacking . . . This duck was sitting out and attacking this guy when he’d go to the mailbox and he wanted the parish to do something about the neighbor’s duck that was attacking him.

1204
01:08:27.983 --> 01:08:30.505
That was a little out there.

1205
01:08:30.505 --> 01:08:33.447
Now look, I’ve had some interesting calls.

1206
01:08:33.447 --> 01:08:40.173
Animal control, a lot of times that’s . . . There’s a lot of interesting things with animal control.

1207
01:08:40.173 --> 01:08:42.275
We run animal control, also.

1208
01:08:42.275 --> 01:08:46.058
And well we got a great guy over there.

1209
01:08:46.058 --> 01:08:49.000
Nathan [Reno?] does a wonderful job.

1210
01:08:49.000 --> 01:08:57.364
What I’ve learned is all my managers at the parish and . . . whether it’s my west side [Darren?] White who’s over my public works,

1211
01:08:57.364 --> 01:09:00.636
or Allen Wayne Wright who’s over entire public works,

1212
01:09:00.636 --> 01:09:07.182
if you empower these people to do their job they, like us, want to do a good job.

1213
01:09:07.182 --> 01:09:15.545
They want to go home in the night and say, “Well I had a good day today I helped a lot of people.”

1214
01:09:15.545 --> 01:09:18.455
So it’s really an opportunity to be there.

1215
01:09:18.455 --> 01:09:26.091
To be that person that has the relationships built inside the police jury whether if it’s from Brian at the top,

1216
01:09:26.091 --> 01:09:33.364
to Dane, to Allen Wayne Wright, or Wes Crane with zoning and planning . . . Being in the mix, I love it.

1217
01:09:33.364 --> 01:09:34.818
I’m in the mix.

1218
01:09:34.818 --> 01:09:36.636
I get to do things.

1219
01:09:36.636 --> 01:09:39.909
I see the inside working of the police jury.

1220
01:09:39.909 --> 01:09:46.091
<v ARSENEAULT>So during hurricane . . . we like to . . . With oral history we like to stay in the far past,

1221
01:09:46.091 --> 01:09:50.818
but I always forget that [Hurricane] Rita was already like ten years ago.

1222
01:09:50.818 --> 01:09:51.182
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah.

1223
01:09:51.182 --> 01:09:53.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So what was that like?

1224
01:09:53.000 --> 01:09:55.909
We can switch gears for just a second.

1225
01:09:55.909 --> 01:09:57.000
<v MCMILLIN>Switch gears?

1226
01:09:57.000 --> 01:09:57.280
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1227
01:09:57.280 --> 01:09:57.560
<v MCMILLIN>Okay.

1228
01:09:57.560 --> 01:09:58.400
Where’s the water?

1229
01:09:58.400 --> 01:10:00.080
I left it in my truck.

1230
01:10:00.080 --> 01:10:00.360
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh.

1231
01:10:00.360 --> 01:10:01.760
<v UNKNOWN>We can take a break.

1232
01:10:01.760 --> 01:10:02.600
<v ARSENEAULT>Take a break?

1233
01:10:02.600 --> 01:10:04.000
Do you have time?

1234
01:10:04.000 --> 01:10:04.312
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah.

1235
01:10:04.312 --> 01:10:06.188
We got time [break in tape].

1236
01:10:06.188 --> 01:10:06.812
<v ARSENEAULT>Talk about . . .

1237
01:10:06.812 --> 01:10:07.125
<v MCMILLIN>Family.

1238
01:10:07.125 --> 01:10:07.750
<v ARSENEAULT>Your siblings.

1239
01:10:07.750 --> 01:10:08.062
<v MCMILLIN>Yes.

1240
01:10:08.062 --> 01:10:10.250
I grew up in a great family.

1241
01:10:10.250 --> 01:10:18.375
I had an older brother of mine that was . . . seventeen years older than me and he lived in the jet set world of New York City.

1242
01:10:18.375 --> 01:10:22.125
Actually, he used to escort Ethel Merman and Broadway starts to stuff.

1243
01:10:22.125 --> 01:10:24.938
So as a young kid I . . . at ten years

1244
01:10:24.938 --> 01:10:28.375
<c.p>old I used to go to New York with my mom.

1245
01:10:28.375 --> 01:10:30.875
My dad never liked to go [on these?].

1246
01:10:30.875 --> 01:10:35.875
So we’d go on trips to New York so [Mackie?] actually died at a young age.

1247
01:10:35.875 --> 01:10:37.125
He died in 1984.

1248
01:10:37.125 --> 01:10:40.562
He was forty-four years old, but he lived a great life.

1249
01:10:40.562 --> 01:10:44.000
He was a publisher for McCall’s books, magazine, then an entrepreneur.

1250
01:10:44.000 --> 01:10:51.500
Started his own business down in Florida a little bit later when he moved out of New York to Florida, but a great guy.

1251
01:10:51.500 --> 01:10:57.750
Brought me into a different culture in the early years of my life that I got to see Broadway plays.

1252
01:10:57.750 --> 01:11:00.562
First Broadway play I ever went to was “Oklahoma.”

1253
01:11:00.562 --> 01:11:06.500
I saw “Mame” and “Jesus Christ Superstar” and this was in the 70s when this was big time stuff.

1254
01:11:06.500 --> 01:11:09.000
So I got to see that aspect.

1255
01:11:09.000 --> 01:11:11.167
My older brother Mickey was a hunter.

1256
01:11:11.167 --> 01:11:14.883
He drug me through the marshes and rice fields of Southwest Louisiana.

1257
01:11:14.883 --> 01:11:16.431
A really good baseball player.

1258
01:11:16.431 --> 01:11:17.979
Always playing ball or something.

1259
01:11:17.979 --> 01:11:19.217
So that was it.

1260
01:11:19.217 --> 01:11:23.861
My sister Jan, who is eight years older than me, sweetest lady in the world.

1261
01:11:23.861 --> 01:11:26.028
She reminds me of my wife Sue.

1262
01:11:26.028 --> 01:11:27.886
Jan’s a lot like Sue; sweetheart.

1263
01:11:27.886 --> 01:11:33.459
I used to . . . she’d have to watch me sometimes and I’d get mad at her and run away.

1264
01:11:33.459 --> 01:11:40.890
We had woods behind the back of our house I’d take my BB gun and go into the woods and take two mayonnaise sandwiches.

1265
01:11:40.890 --> 01:11:42.128
Miracle whip of course.

1266
01:11:42.128 --> 01:11:43.057
Only Miracle Whip.

1267
01:11:43.057 --> 01:11:48.320
Take off and head through the woods and I think she enjoyed me running away I think.

1268
01:11:48.320 --> 01:11:52.655
Before long I’d look around and she had my sandwiches made for me [laughs].

1269
01:11:52.655 --> 01:11:54.822
But we . . . She was eight years older.

1270
01:11:54.822 --> 01:12:01.324
I can remember when she was . . . Let’s see, I guess she would have been about sixteen, starting to date, or seventeen.

1271
01:12:01.324 --> 01:12:02.872
I was eight or nine.

1272
01:12:02.872 --> 01:12:06.278
I would always torture her boyfriends when they would come over.

1273
01:12:06.278 --> 01:12:08.754
She was a homecoming queen at Westlake High.

1274
01:12:08.754 --> 01:12:10.302
I’m so proud of Jan.

1275
01:12:10.302 --> 01:12:12.160
She still lives in Moss Bluff.

1276
01:12:12.160 --> 01:12:13.399
Very proud of her.

1277
01:12:13.399 --> 01:12:17.423
Her grandson by the way is a second baseman for the Atlanta Braves.

1278
01:12:17.423 --> 01:12:18.972
His name is Jayce Peterson.

1279
01:12:18.972 --> 01:12:24.854
So we have a little rock star superstar in the family, but very proud of . . . And she has three

1280
01:12:24.854 --> 01:12:28.260
<c.p>daughters and my brother Mickey has a son and two daughters.

1281
01:12:28.260 --> 01:12:29.498
We’re all very close.

1282
01:12:29.498 --> 01:12:31.046
We got a close family.

1283
01:12:31.046 --> 01:12:36.000
We usually have Christmas at my house and there’s usually sixty or seventy people there.

1284
01:12:36.000 --> 01:12:38.583
I have two children, Marsha and Boon.

1285
01:12:38.583 --> 01:12:43.010
Marsha has a . . . my granddaughter Anne Rose who’s . . . Be two in November.

1286
01:12:43.010 --> 01:12:47.438
And of course Boon has a . . . Some grandchildren there, Hayden and Madison.

1287
01:12:47.438 --> 01:12:50.390
They’re probably . . . Let’s see, eleven and twelve now.

1288
01:12:50.390 --> 01:12:51.497
Madison’s the oldest.

1289
01:12:51.497 --> 01:12:55.186
They’re step-grandkids, but I don’t ever hear the word step.

1290
01:12:55.186 --> 01:12:56.662
The step . . . They’re grandkids.

1291
01:12:56.662 --> 01:13:03.303
I mean like Patrick and Amy who are my step-kids not my birth kids, but they’re my family.

1292
01:13:03.303 --> 01:13:06.993
And I helped raise them and grow up with them.

1293
01:13:06.993 --> 01:13:09.576
We all have a very close relationship.

1294
01:13:09.576 --> 01:13:19.538
In 1995 Sue’s brother and his wife and their son were in a really bad car wreck and it ended up killing Sue’s brother and his wife.

1295
01:13:19.538 --> 01:13:22.490
And Chris [Atchison?] was thirteen at the time.

1296
01:13:22.490 --> 01:13:23.597
An eighth grader.

1297
01:13:23.597 --> 01:13:28.762
Didn’t really know Chris real well, but he was an eighth grader at W.W.

1298
01:13:28.762 --> 01:13:29.131
Lewis.

1299
01:13:29.131 --> 01:13:38.724
But he had just lost his mom and dad and there wasn’t any place for Chris to go so we took him in the McMillin family.

1300
01:13:38.724 --> 01:13:44.997
So I raised Chris from the time he was thirteen, and now he’s a young man grown.

1301
01:13:44.997 --> 01:13:46.472
Has his own family.

1302
01:13:46.472 --> 01:13:47.948
Very proud of Chris.

1303
01:13:47.948 --> 01:13:51.638
He was a kid that made a . . . What’d he make?

1304
01:13:51.638 --> 01:13:54.959
It was twenty-eight or something on the ACT test.

1305
01:13:54.959 --> 01:13:55.697
He’s smart.

1306
01:13:55.697 --> 01:13:59.386
His parents had him really . . . He did well on that.

1307
01:13:59.386 --> 01:14:00.862
He was really smart.

1308
01:14:00.862 --> 01:14:04.552
Got him into high school football and he enjoyed that.

1309
01:14:04.552 --> 01:14:08.610
Although he wasn’t a superstar he hung in with the team.

1310
01:14:08.610 --> 01:14:10.086
He’s got great memories.

1311
01:14:10.086 --> 01:14:17.466
Now he’s a scaffolding supervisor at one of the industries as well as doing real well with the media stuff.

1312
01:14:17.466 --> 01:14:23.000
He’s got drones so he likes to fly and all that kind of stuff.

1313
01:14:23.000 --> 01:14:26.273
So we’ve had a really good family around us.

1314
01:14:26.273 --> 01:14:34.636
And Sue and I have had a blended family, but as I said on the twentieth of July will be our twenty-sixth wedding

1315
01:14:34.636 --> 01:14:35.000
<c.p>anniversary.

1316
01:14:35.000 --> 01:14:38.636
And if you also remember July twentieth another event happened.

1317
01:14:38.636 --> 01:14:41.909
It was the same day as the lunar landing.

1318
01:14:41.909 --> 01:14:44.091
It was on July twentieth 1969.

1319
01:14:44.091 --> 01:14:47.727
So Sue and I have said before we got married,

1320
01:14:47.727 --> 01:14:53.909
“July twentieth it’ll be one small leap for man, one giant leap for Hal and Sue.” [Laughs].

1321
01:14:53.909 --> 01:14:59.727
Anyhow just to coincide with that, it’s always good to get married on some memorable event.

1322
01:14:59.727 --> 01:15:01.909
<v ARSENEAULT>You picked that day on purpose?

1323
01:15:01.909 --> 01:15:05.545
<v MCMILLIN>No, it just happened but it’s the lunar landing so. . .

1324
01:15:05.545 --> 01:15:07.000
<v ARSENEAULT>It’s romantic still . . .

1325
01:15:07.000 --> 01:15:12.156
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah, we always have a great . . . always pick about the same day as the lunar landing.

1326
01:15:12.156 --> 01:15:13.767
But good family growing up.

1327
01:15:13.767 --> 01:15:18.600
Wanted to make sure I got Mom, Dad, Mackie my older brother who passed away.

1328
01:15:18.600 --> 01:15:22.144
Of course Mickey’s still doing well and very active in community.

1329
01:15:22.144 --> 01:15:24.078
Very . . . he’s a retired plumbing contractor.

1330
01:15:24.078 --> 01:15:27.944
He took over my dad’s business after my dad had passed away.

1331
01:15:27.944 --> 01:15:34.389
And one thing that I . . . you talk about life lessons and I’ll try to get through this without choking up,

1332
01:15:34.389 --> 01:15:36.967
but my dad actually died in my arms.

1333
01:15:36.967 --> 01:15:39.867
He had a heart attack and Mom called me.

1334
01:15:39.867 --> 01:15:42.122
We lived around the corner from him.

1335
01:15:42.122 --> 01:15:45.667
That was probably the most major growing event of my life.

1336
01:15:45.667 --> 01:15:47.600
Got over there and did CPR.

1337
01:15:47.600 --> 01:15:53.722
I worked at the plant and you go in for those CPR class and you think you’re not listening,

1338
01:15:53.722 --> 01:15:58.878
but when you have to use it, all that stuff kind of comes back to you.

1339
01:15:58.878 --> 01:16:00.489
He had a heart fibulation.

1340
01:16:00.489 --> 01:16:02.744
He actually probably died on June thirtieth.

1341
01:16:02.744 --> 01:16:08.867
They were able to . . . Because of what we did with the CPR, get his heart back restarted at the

1342
01:16:08.867 --> 01:16:12.411
<c.p>hospital in . . . They unplugged the machine on the fourth of July.

1343
01:16:12.411 --> 01:16:17.567
He didn’t have any EEGs and the doctor, who was a duck hunting buddy of mine,

1344
01:16:17.567 --> 01:16:22.722
a cardiologist named Carl [Fatsterbin?] said, hey look the family doesn’t have to make any decisions.

1345
01:16:22.722 --> 01:16:26.911
I know what my dad would have wanted, who was a hard-working guy.

1346
01:16:26.911 --> 01:16:32.389
In fact, the day he passed away that Saturday night, he had roughed in a couple houses.

1347
01:16:32.389 --> 01:16:35.289
Him and Ernest had roughed in a couple houses.

1348
01:16:35.289 --> 01:16:41.733
He had a good day because he loved to work and he kind of died in his boots that night.

1349
01:16:41.733 --> 01:16:51.722
But that was probably the number one most biggest growing experience that I’ve ever had in my life because it taught me how short life is and how final death is.

1350
01:16:51.722 --> 01:17:01.389
That was kind of the moment that I started changing a few things in my life and started pursuing things that I really wanted to do or I hadn’t done;

1351
01:17:01.389 --> 01:17:02.356
been putting off.

1352
01:17:02.356 --> 01:17:03.000
So . . .

1353
01:17:03.000 --> 01:17:04.682
<v ARSENEAULT>How old were you?

1354
01:17:04.682 --> 01:17:05.102
<v MCMILLIN>Twenty-eight.

1355
01:17:05.102 --> 01:17:06.784
I was twenty-eight, yeah.

1356
01:17:06.784 --> 01:17:08.045
So big moments.

1357
01:17:08.045 --> 01:17:09.307
Later things change.

1358
01:17:09.307 --> 01:17:11.409
Went through divorce after that.

1359
01:17:11.409 --> 01:17:12.670
Worked at Sasol.

1360
01:17:12.670 --> 01:17:14.352
That was in ’85.

1361
01:17:14.352 --> 01:17:17.295
I stayed there a few more years.

1362
01:17:17.295 --> 01:17:21.920
Kind of said, hey I’ve enjoyed this working in the plants.

1363
01:17:21.920 --> 01:17:27.386
Now I want to pursue something that I could do outside the plants.

1364
01:17:27.386 --> 01:17:30.750
And that’s where I went into business development.

1365
01:17:30.750 --> 01:17:33.273
But that was a big moment.

1366
01:17:33.273 --> 01:17:36.636
Lost my mom February twenty-eighth of this year.

1367
01:17:36.636 --> 01:17:38.739
She was ninety-three years old.

1368
01:17:38.739 --> 01:17:40.841
Her body just wore out.

1369
01:17:40.841 --> 01:17:42.943
It was just the situation.

1370
01:17:42.943 --> 01:17:51.352
The greatest mom in the world just . . . At a point in time that it almost gets to where you say,

1371
01:17:51.352 --> 01:17:53.875
“Okay Lord I know she’s tired.

1372
01:17:53.875 --> 01:17:55.977
Let her go see Dad.”

1373
01:17:55.977 --> 01:18:09.432
So I guess really the biggest thing . . . I am spiritual so I know that they’re in heaven and I’ll see them one day hopefully if I mind my P’s and Q’s right.

1374
01:18:09.432 --> 01:18:13.216
But the good Lord will take care of us

1375
01:18:13.216 --> 01:18:17.000
<c.p>and I just miss both of them, so.

1376
01:18:17.000 --> 01:18:18.674
<v ARSENEAULT>They raised you in church?

1377
01:18:18.674 --> 01:18:19.008
<v MCMILLIN>Yes.

1378
01:18:19.008 --> 01:18:25.033
Raised in a Baptist church, later when I got married, Methodist church and I’ve been Methodist since, so.

1379
01:18:25.033 --> 01:18:31.059
And then Mom and Dad went in the Catholic church and they were both active in that, so.

1380
01:18:31.059 --> 01:18:31.728
Good people.

1381
01:18:31.728 --> 01:18:36.749
And my sister Jan, she’s the salt of the earth when it comes to church.

1382
01:18:36.749 --> 01:18:38.088
She doesn’t miss church.

1383
01:18:38.088 --> 01:18:40.096
She prays for us every day.

1384
01:18:40.096 --> 01:18:42.439
We all pray, but not like Jan.

1385
01:18:42.439 --> 01:18:43.444
Jan prays constantly.

1386
01:18:43.444 --> 01:18:44.782
She’s a tremendous person.

1387
01:18:44.782 --> 01:18:48.130
Of course Mickey is also . . . We’re all good Christian background.

1388
01:18:48.130 --> 01:18:57.167
We all try to do the right thing and we all know there is a hereafter and there is . . . we . . . I believe Jesus died for our sins,

1389
01:18:57.167 --> 01:19:04.531
and he was the son of God, and that we’re all going to have an opportunity to go to heaven someday, so.

1390
01:19:04.531 --> 01:19:05.870
Look forward to that.

1391
01:19:05.870 --> 01:19:08.883
I got a lot of friends up there already.

1392
01:19:08.883 --> 01:19:12.565
That’s one thing about Westlake being a hometown, you know everybody.

1393
01:19:12.565 --> 01:19:17.251
I often say you know whose checks are good and whose husbands aren’t [laughs].

1394
01:19:17.251 --> 01:19:24.950
So that’s a hometown deal, but it’s kind of one of those things that . . . A good part about growing up in a community.

1395
01:19:24.950 --> 01:19:33.318
Just like Ms. Trahan knew I threw tomatoes at her car, people know your business to a degree, but there’s plusses and minuses with that.

1396
01:19:33.318 --> 01:19:35.326
I mean I love my hometown.

1397
01:19:35.326 --> 01:19:37.000
It’s a great place.

1398
01:19:37.000 --> 01:19:41.837
<v ARSENEAULT>Just curious before we get to Hurricane Rita, what do you know about Gerald Washington’s death?

1399
01:19:41.837 --> 01:19:50.000
This is something . . . I’m not from the area and it’s something I kind of . . . I did interview Mrs. Lillian Washington because her children went to Mossville.

1400
01:19:50.000 --> 01:19:52.688
<v MCMILLIN>Gerald Washington was one of my closest friends.

1401
01:19:52.688 --> 01:19:57.727
Gerald Washington and I got elected to the Westlake city council at the same time.

1402
01:19:57.727 --> 01:19:58.735
We were brothers.

1403
01:19:58.735 --> 01:20:00.415
I was excited for Gerald.

1404
01:20:00.415 --> 01:20:04.782
Gerald and I would go on . . . they would think we were twins, okay?

1405
01:20:04.782 --> 01:20:09.485
This is what I know about Gerald Washington and how much I loved him.

1406
01:20:09.485 --> 01:20:13.517
This guy, also a great supervisor out at Conoco, Phillips 66 now.

1407
01:20:13.517 --> 01:20:14.525
Everybody loved Gerald.

1408
01:20:14.525 --> 01:20:18.556
We played on the same Conoco basketball team together . . . We were close.

1409
01:20:18.556 --> 01:20:25.275
We would go on conventions as city councilmen and everybody knew me and Gerald because we were both people persons.

1410
01:20:25.275 --> 01:20:26.619
We talked to everybody.

1411
01:20:26.619 --> 01:20:33.338
He said I promised duck hunts to people all over the world, but we’d find a common bond with them.

1412
01:20:33.338 --> 01:20:40.057
He taught me no matter how big the convention is and how great this speaker is, go to the front.

1413
01:20:40.057 --> 01:20:43.081
There will always be a couple seats up there.

1414
01:20:43.081 --> 01:20:46.776
Me and Wash saw Vice President Gore, we saw Dick Cheney,

1415
01:20:46.776 --> 01:20:52.151
and we were always within the first one or two rows because we’d find a seat.

1416
01:20:52.151 --> 01:20:56.519
There’d be maybe one seat here, one . . . We’d ask people to push together.

1417
01:20:56.519 --> 01:20:59.206
Me and Wash would be sitting up there.

1418
01:20:59.206 --> 01:21:00.214
We were inseparable.

1419
01:21:00.214 --> 01:21:01.558
We were great friends.

1420
01:21:01.558 --> 01:21:07.941
The morning that . . . The day that he died I saw him that morning at the convenience store, “Hey baby.”

1421
01:21:07.941 --> 01:21:08.613
“Hey Mac.”

1422
01:21:08.613 --> 01:21:09.621
“How’s it going?”

1423
01:21:09.621 --> 01:21:10.965
I said, “Good, Wash.”

1424
01:21:10.965 --> 01:21:14.324
I said, “I can’t wait for you to be mayor.”

1425
01:21:14.324 --> 01:21:17.348
He’d just gotten elected mayor and stuff like this.

1426
01:21:17.348 --> 01:21:22.051
You could have gave me a list of a hundred people, or ten, whatever.

1427
01:21:22.051 --> 01:21:25.411
Wash would have been on the bottom of that list.

1428
01:21:25.411 --> 01:21:30.786
He would . . . I would have never thought that he would have committed suicide, but he did.

1429
01:21:30.786 --> 01:21:38.849
He had things undoubtedly that was bothering him that he felt like he needed to check out at the point and time he did.

1430
01:21:38.849 --> 01:21:39.857
Broke my heart.

1431
01:21:39.857 --> 01:21:43.216
I was laying on the couch watching a UFC fight.

1432
01:21:43.216 --> 01:21:46.912
Buddy of mine called me said, “Hey man Wash is dead.

1433
01:21:46.912 --> 01:21:47.919
He killed himself.”

1434
01:21:47.919 --> 01:21:50.607
I’m like, “No.” I said, “Where’s Mary at?”

1435
01:21:50.607 --> 01:21:51.279
Because Mary

1436
01:21:51.279 --> 01:21:53.295
<c.p>was a good friend of ours.

1437
01:21:53.295 --> 01:21:56.654
I said, “Where’s Mary at?” he said, “Mossville Recreation Center.”

1438
01:21:56.654 --> 01:21:59.006
Well she wasn’t at Mossville Recreation Center,

1439
01:21:59.006 --> 01:22:10.428
she was at her house but I was going to the Mossville Recreation Center when I came by the Mossville School and saw Wash’s body laying out there in the coroner in the police.

1440
01:22:10.428 --> 01:22:18.827
So I stopped and visited with the mayor and some of the city councilmen, the fire chief, because we’re all just like, “Oh my gosh.”

1441
01:22:18.827 --> 01:22:21.851
I was on the police jury at that time.

1442
01:22:21.851 --> 01:22:26.218
I wasn’t [already a?] city councilman, but Wash had helped me get elected.

1443
01:22:26.218 --> 01:22:28.234
Wash was my right hand guy.

1444
01:22:28.234 --> 01:22:29.914
I mean I love Wash.

1445
01:22:29.914 --> 01:22:30.921
We were buddies.

1446
01:22:30.921 --> 01:22:31.929
We were friends.

1447
01:22:31.929 --> 01:22:35.625
He was somebody . . . a man of integrity that I could trust.

1448
01:22:35.625 --> 01:22:41.000
If I had something point-counterpoint with I could get Wash’s opinion I always loved it.

1449
01:22:41.000 --> 01:22:48.629
There was conspiracy theories and different things, but if you follow the blood it was a shot to the chest,

1450
01:22:48.629 --> 01:22:56.258
he went down, he got up, he moved, and the two different pools of blood were there, and he died.

1451
01:22:56.258 --> 01:22:59.309
Why he did it haunts all of us.

1452
01:22:59.309 --> 01:23:00.454
I don’t know.

1453
01:23:00.454 --> 01:23:01.598
I don’t know.

1454
01:23:01.598 --> 01:23:08.845
I hope when I see him in heaven I’m going to ask him, “Hey man why’d you do that?

1455
01:23:08.845 --> 01:23:10.371
You left me hanging.

1456
01:23:10.371 --> 01:23:11.515
I needed you.”

1457
01:23:11.515 --> 01:23:13.041
But a great man.

1458
01:23:13.041 --> 01:23:18.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Why do you think it was at . . . this is speculation, but I’m . . .?

1459
01:23:18.000 --> 01:23:18.727
<v MCMILLIN>Who knows.

1460
01:23:18.727 --> 01:23:19.818
At the school?

1461
01:23:19.818 --> 01:23:20.182
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1462
01:23:20.182 --> 01:23:20.909
<v MCMILLIN>Common place.

1463
01:23:20.909 --> 01:23:22.000
I don’t know.

1464
01:23:22.000 --> 01:23:27.091
I don’t know probably some . . . meant something . . . I mean that was his high school.

1465
01:23:27.091 --> 01:23:30.000
I mean it’s where he went to school.

1466
01:23:30.000 --> 01:23:34.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Maybe remembering the good old days or something like that.

1467
01:23:34.000 --> 01:23:35.167
<v MCMILLIN>I don’t know.

1468
01:23:35.167 --> 01:23:43.333
I don’t know why he just stopped his truck out there and did it . . . Just took his pistol and did it.

1469
01:23:43.333 --> 01:23:53.056
But I was the only guy see being comfortable with all . . . people of all walks of life and especially I . . . after I went by there,

1470
01:23:53.056 --> 01:23:57.333
I left there and I went to Wash and Mary’s house.

1471
01:23:57.333 --> 01:24:05.889
I was the only white guy there, but I was family because Wash was my best friend and I cried with Mary,

1472
01:24:05.889 --> 01:24:09.778
and cried with his mom, and the whole family, so.

1473
01:24:09.778 --> 01:24:11.333
I don’t know why.

1474
01:24:11.333 --> 01:24:12.111
That’s . . . what . . .

1475
01:24:12.111 --> 01:24:14.444
<v ARSENEAULT>How did the family take it?

1476
01:24:14.444 --> 01:24:16.000
<v MCMILLIN>It was killing them.

1477
01:24:16.000 --> 01:24:17.167
It killed everybody.

1478
01:24:17.167 --> 01:24:19.500
I mean everybody was so upset.

1479
01:24:19.500 --> 01:24:20.667
Nobody knew why.

1480
01:24:20.667 --> 01:24:23.000
Nobody . . . Why is this . . . it’s tragic.

1481
01:24:23.000 --> 01:24:24.556
I mean sudden loss.

1482
01:24:24.556 --> 01:24:31.167
I was up until two o’clock in the morning, three o’clock in the morning with those guys.

1483
01:24:31.167 --> 01:24:33.889
Sitting there and just don’t know why.

1484
01:24:33.889 --> 01:24:39.722
It was rumors somebody did it, conspiracy theories, and I don’t believe any of that.

1485
01:24:39.722 --> 01:24:49.056
It was a suicide that he decided that his own time to do it his way and check out the way he wanted to.

1486
01:24:49.056 --> 01:24:50.611
I don’t know why.

1487
01:24:50.611 --> 01:24:52.944
But what a flamboyant good speaker.

1488
01:24:52.944 --> 01:24:56.444
He walked into a room, he’d light it up.

1489
01:24:56.444 --> 01:25:00.333
I mean he was six-seven, six-eight . . . good guy . . . big guy.

1490
01:25:00.333 --> 01:25:08.889
Me and him had a lot of drinks together, played a lot of cards at the casino together, we’d go on trips.

1491
01:25:08.889 --> 01:25:15.500
And went to all the classes, and all the conventions, and heard a lot of great speakers.

1492
01:25:15.500 --> 01:25:17.833
That’s a hole in my heart.

1493
01:25:17.833 --> 01:25:19.000
Good guy.

1494
01:25:19.000 --> 01:25:20.960
<v ARSENEAULT>Well tell us a little about Rita.

1495
01:25:20.960 --> 01:25:22.360
I know [Hurricane] Katrina gets

1496
01:25:22.360 --> 01:25:23.480
<c.p>a lot of attention.

1497
01:25:23.480 --> 01:25:23.760
<v MCMILLIN>Well . . .

1498
01:25:23.760 --> 01:25:26.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Calcasieu Parish got it hard with Rita.

1499
01:25:26.000 --> 01:25:29.982
<v MCMILLIN>Here’s the deal it was . . . I was president of the police jury.

1500
01:25:29.982 --> 01:25:30.977
Had that opportunity.

1501
01:25:30.977 --> 01:25:35.622
I’ve served twice as president of the police jury and [now?] as vice president.

1502
01:25:35.622 --> 01:25:44.249
I’m vice president this year, but president of the police jury when Hurricane Katrina hit and we really mobilized to help the evacuees in New Orleans.

1503
01:25:44.249 --> 01:25:46.571
We had a lot of them here.

1504
01:25:46.571 --> 01:25:53.207
We had a lot of them at Burton Coliseum and we had a lot of them at the Civic Center.

1505
01:25:53.207 --> 01:25:58.848
And the parish [on at?] Burton we kind of made sure that facility . . . We were feeding people,

1506
01:25:58.848 --> 01:26:04.488
we were housing people, we were taking care of their needs and doing a really good job.

1507
01:26:04.488 --> 01:26:14.111
And they were loving it because this is a good part of the world and our people had open hearts and they were really helping the people of Katrina.

1508
01:26:14.111 --> 01:26:23.401
And then Rita comes in to play and we have to evacuate all of those people out of the Burton Coliseum and they have to go different places,

1509
01:26:23.401 --> 01:26:25.392
and out of the Civic Center.

1510
01:26:25.392 --> 01:26:29.373
Evacuate them and then we had to start getting ready for evacuation.

1511
01:26:29.373 --> 01:26:31.032
Here’s what I didn’t know.

1512
01:26:31.032 --> 01:26:38.000
The president of the police jury becomes the highest ranking public official in a declared national disaster in the parish.

1513
01:26:38.000 --> 01:26:39.729
<v ARSENEAULT>Is it usually the mayor?

1514
01:26:39.729 --> 01:26:46.990
<v MCMILLIN>No, it’s the president of the police jury during a parish wide declared national disaster the president of the police becomes . . .

1515
01:26:46.990 --> 01:26:47.336
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1516
01:26:47.336 --> 01:26:54.942
<v MCMILLIN>So I’m a part time elected official, but it suddenly thrust me into being the guy in the control of the meetings.

1517
01:26:54.942 --> 01:26:58.400
Fortunately, we had six great mayors from six great cities.

1518
01:26:58.400 --> 01:26:59.783
They were full time.

1519
01:26:59.783 --> 01:27:03.932
Some of them were full time, some of them were part time.

1520
01:27:03.932 --> 01:27:09.464
But Mayor Roach and I really became close during that because Randy’s a wealth of information,

1521
01:27:09.464 --> 01:27:13.268
an eloquent speaker, and is all about doing the right thing.

1522
01:27:13.268 --> 01:27:19.146
I was a part time elected official, and having to lead the meetings would lead and listen,

1523
01:27:19.146 --> 01:27:31.247
but I would also take charge of the meetings to make sure that we . . . I guess it was a great learning curve for me that when we were having the meeting nobody spoke over anyone.

1524
01:27:31.247 --> 01:27:33.322
We’d call that to order quickly.

1525
01:27:33.322 --> 01:27:38.163
Make sure that everybody’s point . . . I kind of talk about that in the beginning.

1526
01:27:38.163 --> 01:27:45.424
That everybody’s point was very important and we need to listen to what everyone’s point was, and please no sidebar conversations.

1527
01:27:45.424 --> 01:27:50.956
And somebody was side barring on my conversations when we were making some really tough decisions.

1528
01:27:50.956 --> 01:27:52.339
We’d shut that down.

1529
01:27:52.339 --> 01:27:58.217
Say, “Hey I appreciate what you’re saying over there, but we need to listen to whoever’s speaking.”

1530
01:27:58.217 --> 01:28:00.292
And we got in these rooms.

1531
01:28:00.292 --> 01:28:07.553
The core group of the mayors and the police jury and we had to make some tough decisions, but we did.

1532
01:28:07.553 --> 01:28:10.664
And we all point-counterpointed at those point in times,

1533
01:28:10.664 --> 01:28:20.000
but when we came out of there we came out of there in unity because the citizens of Calcasieu Parish needed decision makers to make decisions.

1534
01:28:20.000 --> 01:28:22.423
<v ARSENEAULT>What were some of the tough ones?

1535
01:28:22.423 --> 01:28:26.577
<v MCMILLIN>Well, initially closing the schools and calling for a parish wide evacuation.

1536
01:28:26.577 --> 01:28:29.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Because you didn’t know if it?

1537
01:28:29.000 --> 01:28:30.307
<v MCMILLIN>You don’t know.

1538
01:28:30.307 --> 01:28:38.587
It’s kind of bumping along the Texas southern coast and is it going to turn, or is it not?

1539
01:28:38.587 --> 01:28:42.073
Well let’s err on the side of safety.

1540
01:28:42.073 --> 01:28:43.816
<v ARSENEAULT>And that’s a decision . . .

1541
01:28:43.816 --> 01:28:50.352
<v MCMILLIN>That was a decision that was made . . . It was made finally by the schoolboard superintendent,

1542
01:28:50.352 --> 01:28:55.581
but that was a decision that was discussed amongst all of us.

1543
01:28:55.581 --> 01:28:59.067
Said, “Yeah it’s the right thing to do.”

1544
01:28:59.067 --> 01:29:01.682
Then we all went on T.V.

1545
01:29:01.682 --> 01:29:04.732
Said, “Hey guys, you saw what happened.”

1546
01:29:04.732 --> 01:29:14.754
I guess that’s one of the great things that we had was people three weeks earlier had seen what happened in Hurricane Katrina.

1547
01:29:14.754 --> 01:29:19.983
So we call for mandatory evacuation of Calcasieu Parish [break in tape].

1548
01:29:19.983 --> 01:29:22.162
Okay go back over that.

1549
01:29:22.162 --> 01:29:26.520
They saw what happened three weeks earlier in Hurricane Katrina.

1550
01:29:26.520 --> 01:29:31.749
When we called for mandatory evacuation in Calcasieu Parish the people listened.

1551
01:29:31.749 --> 01:29:33.927
They did and they evacuated.

1552
01:29:33.927 --> 01:29:38.285
Now, it was tough because people didn’t want to evacuate.

1553
01:29:38.285 --> 01:29:40.899
It’s hard to leave your home.

1554
01:29:40.899 --> 01:29:43.514
Hard to leave all your possessions.

1555
01:29:43.514 --> 01:29:47.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How do you evacuate that many people?

1556
01:29:47.000 --> 01:29:47.356
<v MCMILLIN>Yeah.

1557
01:29:47.356 --> 01:29:48.782
That was the thing.

1558
01:29:48.782 --> 01:29:58.050
And there was gridlock and there was . . . Could have been done better with contraflow and a lot of things in hindsight we could have done better,

1559
01:29:58.050 --> 01:30:03.040
but it’d been forty-seven years, since 1957 that we had not had a hurricane.

1560
01:30:03.040 --> 01:30:04.822
So Hurricane Audrey last . . . Cameron.

1561
01:30:04.822 --> 01:30:06.248
So we didn’t know.

1562
01:30:06.248 --> 01:30:13.020
We were listening, but we had saw what happened to Katrina and it scared the heck out of us.

1563
01:30:13.020 --> 01:30:15.515
So, alright we’re going to get ready.

1564
01:30:15.515 --> 01:30:17.653
We went to evacuate, everybody’s together.

1565
01:30:17.653 --> 01:30:18.723
We do that.

1566
01:30:18.723 --> 01:30:19.792
It hit us.

1567
01:30:19.792 --> 01:30:20.505
Direct hit.

1568
01:30:20.505 --> 01:30:23.000
Hurricane Rita was a direct hit.

1569
01:30:23.000 --> 01:30:25.694
<v ARSENEAULT>Were y’all still in town when it . . .?

1570
01:30:25.694 --> 01:30:26.849
<v MCMILLIN>No, I evacuated.

1571
01:30:26.849 --> 01:30:30.698
I actually evacuated . . . my cousin’s place up above Natchez, Mississippi.

1572
01:30:30.698 --> 01:30:34.162
They told me . . . Said, “Hal go ahead and go.

1573
01:30:34.162 --> 01:30:36.086
We’ll get you back in.”

1574
01:30:36.086 --> 01:30:37.625
So some people stayed.

1575
01:30:37.625 --> 01:30:46.863
The OEP [Office of Emergency Preparedness] department stayed, some of the sheriff department stayed, some of the people that didn’t want to evacuate stayed.

1576
01:30:46.863 --> 01:30:53.405
Everybody I talked to that should have evacuated and didn’t said, “That’ll never happen to me again.

1577
01:30:53.405 --> 01:30:56.869
That was the most scariest time in my life.

1578
01:30:56.869 --> 01:30:58.794
I feared for my life.

1579
01:30:58.794 --> 01:31:04.182
They said that the howling of the wind got loud, and louder, and louder.

1580
01:31:04.182 --> 01:31:13.804
It was the scariest situation with trees snapping, and wind blowing, and not knowing if a hurricane was going to come right through whatever, so.

1581
01:31:13.804 --> 01:31:14.959
It was devastating.

1582
01:31:14.959 --> 01:31:22.271
The aftermath after the hurricane came through, I came back that Sunday about noon I made it in Lafayette.

1583
01:31:22.271 --> 01:31:24.966
And then we came from Lafayette here.

1584
01:31:24.966 --> 01:31:31.124
I was up in Jonesville area, north Louisiana Natchez Jonesville driving home, or driving to Lafayette.

1585
01:31:31.124 --> 01:31:32.663
I was pretty nervous.

1586
01:31:32.663 --> 01:31:38.052
I was in some squall lines that had fifty, sixty mile an hour winds.

1587
01:31:38.052 --> 01:31:45.749
I was thinking, “Wow maybe I left a little too soon” because that storm kind of moved up through there.

1588
01:31:45.749 --> 01:31:46.134
Getting

1589
01:31:46.134 --> 01:31:54.601
<c.p>home, or getting back to southwest Louisiana and seeing the impact that Hurricane Rita had done just driving was . . . Oh, my gosh.

1590
01:31:54.601 --> 01:31:56.526
It looked like a warzone.

1591
01:31:56.526 --> 01:31:58.835
We had been tore up bad.

1592
01:31:58.835 --> 01:32:07.687
The numbers of . . . In hindsight with all the numbers that came in, but the numbers of debris from the old growth oak trees,

1593
01:32:07.687 --> 01:32:15.000
and pine trees, and all the trees we had with the direct hit from Hurricane Rita was tremendous.

1594
01:32:15.000 --> 01:32:18.810
Now, my house and my mother’s house both got totaled.

1595
01:32:18.810 --> 01:32:23.000
I had an oak tree in the middle of my house.

1596
01:32:23.000 --> 01:32:24.905
I completely lost my house.

1597
01:32:24.905 --> 01:32:26.810
My mom lost her house.

1598
01:32:26.810 --> 01:32:32.524
She was this single . . . no, she had remarried my step-dad at that point in time,

1599
01:32:32.524 --> 01:32:34.810
but they . . . Her house was devastated.

1600
01:32:34.810 --> 01:32:42.429
So not only coming back to this massive hurricane that just did Cameron and Calcasieu Parish and all southwest Louisiana,

1601
01:32:42.429 --> 01:32:53.857
but then to deal with a home that was destroyed and then the parish was in such bad shape that we had to do a kind of a lock down.

1602
01:32:53.857 --> 01:32:58.048
We asked some people not to come back to Calcasieu Parish.

1603
01:32:58.048 --> 01:33:05.286
That we’re going to have to ask you not to come back until we can get our infrastructure back.

1604
01:33:05.286 --> 01:33:12.905
Because if you come back now, we don’t have our hospitals, we don’t have our emergency ambulances and things . . . firetrucks.

1605
01:33:12.905 --> 01:33:16.714
All our systems were down at this point in time.

1606
01:33:16.714 --> 01:33:22.810
So it was kind of a situation that we asked the people not to come back.

1607
01:33:22.810 --> 01:33:26.619
To kind of have a . . . To close the parish basically.

1608
01:33:26.619 --> 01:33:34.238
And then we started letting people with IDs come back in to rebuild the parish to get things back up.

1609
01:33:34.238 --> 01:33:38.048
You had the industries that wanted to get back up.

1610
01:33:38.048 --> 01:33:41.476
Check out their damage, get everything going back up.

1611
01:33:41.476 --> 01:33:46.048
You had the small businesses, community stores, wanting to get back up.

1612
01:33:46.048 --> 01:33:50.619
We needed fuel, we needed those kind of stores coming back up.

1613
01:33:50.619 --> 01:33:52.143
We started having meetings.

1614
01:33:52.143 --> 01:33:53.286
Working with that.

1615
01:33:53.286 --> 01:34:07.000
I can tell you that the federal government because of the kind of egg on their face they had during Hurricane Katrina wanted to make sure they were here the day . . . right . . . that I was . . .

1616
01:34:07.000 --> 01:34:09.304
<v ARSENEAULT>You mean FEMA [Federal Emergency Management Agency]?

1617
01:34:09.304 --> 01:34:10.949
<v MCMILLIN>Well yeah, everybody from there.

1618
01:34:10.949 --> 01:34:12.595
General Honoré was here immediately.

1619
01:34:12.595 --> 01:34:16.215
And him and I became pretty good friends during Hurricane Rita.

1620
01:34:16.215 --> 01:34:23.127
Had a number of meetings with him, but he was helping a lot with the cattle down in Calcasieu . . . Cameron Parish.

1621
01:34:23.127 --> 01:34:24.114
Getting those up.

1622
01:34:24.114 --> 01:34:26.747
Getting Hackberry area that had been hit hard.

1623
01:34:26.747 --> 01:34:28.392
Cameron had been hit hard.

1624
01:34:28.392 --> 01:34:33.329
He was some of that, but all up in Calcasieu Parish we would have meetings.

1625
01:34:33.329 --> 01:34:37.278
We had a wealth of resources from the federal government coming in.

1626
01:34:37.278 --> 01:34:39.911
Water and fuel, were the two main things.

1627
01:34:39.911 --> 01:34:42.215
I remember General Honoré saying, “President McMillin?”

1628
01:34:42.215 --> 01:34:43.532
I did his impersonation.

1629
01:34:43.532 --> 01:34:50.114
He said, “President McMillin,” he said, “I’m going to tell you right now,” he said, “My men understand two things.

1630
01:34:50.114 --> 01:34:52.418
They understand order, and they understand rank.

1631
01:34:52.418 --> 01:34:58.342
If you need something President McMillin you tell them who you are and you give them the order.”

1632
01:34:58.342 --> 01:35:00.646
And I’m like, oh my gosh [laughs].

1633
01:35:00.646 --> 01:35:06.899
I got a general . . . A three star general in the United States Army telling me to order people around.

1634
01:35:06.899 --> 01:35:13.152
I’m like, “Well thank you general I appreciate it, but I’m probably going to go to some army colonel,

1635
01:35:13.152 --> 01:35:17.101
or lieutenant, or captain, or whatever and get them to do it.”

1636
01:35:17.101 --> 01:35:19.734
But he was . . . he’s like, “Whatever you need.”

1637
01:35:19.734 --> 01:35:22.696
He said, “We’re going to make this thing work.”

1638
01:35:22.696 --> 01:35:25.000
And he was on the spot.

1639
01:35:25.000 --> 01:35:31.095
And then of course the senators and the congressmen came in that Tuesday after Hurricane Rita.

1640
01:35:31.095 --> 01:35:33.762
We had a meeting with President Bush.

1641
01:35:33.762 --> 01:35:49.000
And that was probably one of the most surreal things in my life because you . . . Three weeks earlier you’re seeing President Bush walking through New Orleans and the aftermath . . . Meeting with the governor and stuff in New Orleans with Katrina.

1642
01:35:49.000 --> 01:35:54.714
And then now he’s here in Lake Charles and you’re in the meeting with him.

1643
01:35:54.714 --> 01:35:55.095
So . . .

1644
01:35:55.095 --> 01:35:57.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What was that like?

1645
01:35:57.000 --> 01:35:57.701
<v MCMILLIN>That was . . .

1646
01:35:57.701 --> 01:35:59.103
<v ARSENEAULT>What was he like?

1647
01:35:59.103 --> 01:35:59.454
<v MCMILLIN>Great.

1648
01:35:59.454 --> 01:35:59.804
Great.

1649
01:35:59.804 --> 01:36:01.206
I love George Bush.

1650
01:36:01.206 --> 01:36:04.010
He was like you had just got whipped,

1651
01:36:04.010 --> 01:36:11.021
but your big brother just walked in the room and he was fixing to take care of business for you.

1652
01:36:11.021 --> 01:36:14.526
It was a good feeling to see President Bush there.

1653
01:36:14.526 --> 01:36:19.784
I had . . . The night before he was coming in, [Mark?] Murry was our parish administrator.

1654
01:36:19.784 --> 01:36:25.041
Of course we had a good group of people from all the parish right there.

1655
01:36:25.041 --> 01:36:28.546
It had a good group of people from the parish.

1656
01:36:28.546 --> 01:36:30.299
So I had a pre-meeting.

1657
01:36:30.299 --> 01:36:32.052
I said, “Look,” I said,

1658
01:36:32.052 --> 01:36:40.113
“I’m going to be in this meeting with the president and although I may have been the class clown and comic in school,

1659
01:36:40.113 --> 01:36:45.021
I’ve learned that I need to get real serious on some of this stuff.

1660
01:36:45.021 --> 01:36:52.031
So make this C student in high school look like I was a banner roll or honor roll student.” [Laughs].

1661
01:36:52.031 --> 01:36:54.134
They said, “Don’t worry about it.

1662
01:36:54.134 --> 01:36:56.938
President Bush was a C student.” [Both laugh].

1663
01:36:56.938 --> 01:37:00.093
I said, “Well good we’ll have a great rapport.”

1664
01:37:00.093 --> 01:37:02.196
No, he was very, very smart.

1665
01:37:02.196 --> 01:37:07.682
He was very, very . . . it was just . . . To tell you a few things about that.

1666
01:37:07.682 --> 01:37:14.723
We had some major power lines down, we had some major infrastructure road issues, things that we needed to get done.

1667
01:37:14.723 --> 01:37:21.763
We needed more fuel, more water, we needed to get more utility companies in here not only on the major stuff,

1668
01:37:21.763 --> 01:37:24.110
but on the local poles and things.

1669
01:37:24.110 --> 01:37:36.514
Electricity is a big part of getting that back up and Entergy did a tremendous job in coming in and really handling up on our problems that we had after being strained through the New Orleans stuff.

1670
01:37:36.514 --> 01:37:39.197
But our public works department, all the fire

1671
01:37:39.197 --> 01:37:42.549
<c.p>departments, the police departments . . . we all pulled as one together.

1672
01:37:42.549 --> 01:37:49.925
And we as leaders had an opportunity to lead, and that’s something . . . I’m very proud to be a part of that team.

1673
01:37:49.925 --> 01:37:51.601
That we did make decisions.

1674
01:37:51.601 --> 01:38:03.000
And sometimes you base those decisions on well let’s learn from past mistakes and there had been a few mistakes in Katrina so we didn’t want to make any of those mistakes twice.

1675
01:38:03.000 --> 01:38:11.832
So we would take all the information we had and we made a lot of right decisions in getting things done to get this parish back up and moving forward.

1676
01:38:11.832 --> 01:38:13.893
We were down . . . We were knocked down.

1677
01:38:13.893 --> 01:38:19.486
Like I told President Bush, “We’re knocked to our knees right now,” I said, “but we’ll get back up.

1678
01:38:19.486 --> 01:38:20.369
We’ll start walking.

1679
01:38:20.369 --> 01:38:21.841
We’ll be running before long.”

1680
01:38:21.841 --> 01:38:27.140
But I was . . . it was a horseshoe . . . Kind of a square with the end out, let’s just say.

1681
01:38:27.140 --> 01:38:31.556
And president Bush was right here and Dave Paulison the FEMA director was right here,

1682
01:38:31.556 --> 01:38:35.089
and I was sitting right here one chair away from the president.

1683
01:38:35.089 --> 01:38:36.561
And man, you know me.

1684
01:38:36.561 --> 01:38:38.033
I like to impersonate people.

1685
01:38:38.033 --> 01:38:44.804
And I always liked the George Bush impersonations, but I wasn’t going to do my George Bush impersonation, but I was watching him.

1686
01:38:44.804 --> 01:38:48.631
And when he would talk and he would move I was thinking, “Wow.

1687
01:38:48.631 --> 01:38:52.164
That guy is doing the greatest George Bush impersonation I’ve ever seen.”

1688
01:38:52.164 --> 01:38:55.107
And I thought, “Oh my God that is George Bush!

1689
01:38:55.107 --> 01:38:55.991
He’s right on!”

1690
01:38:55.991 --> 01:39:01.584
He was the president of the United States sitting one chair away from me and I was like, “Wow.

1691
01:39:01.584 --> 01:39:02.467
What a moment.”

1692
01:39:02.467 --> 01:39:04.234
This . . . it’s just this doesn’t happen.

1693
01:39:04.234 --> 01:39:05.706
It doesn’t happen to me.

1694
01:39:05.706 --> 01:39:06.294
It’s happening.

1695
01:39:06.294 --> 01:39:06.883
It’s surreal.

1696
01:39:06.883 --> 01:39:08.650
And Dave Paulison is right here.

1697
01:39:08.650 --> 01:39:12.182
And of course we were talking expedited assistance and stuff like this.

1698
01:39:12.182 --> 01:39:16.009
And the conversation started out, and it started going to the president’s right.

1699
01:39:16.009 --> 01:39:22.192
And the governor was there, and the senator was there, and the congressman was there, and some other mayors were there.

1700
01:39:22.192 --> 01:39:25.136
So it started going that way, and I’m thinking, “Wow.

1701
01:39:25.136 --> 01:39:28.374
I’ve got a whole list of things here that I need

1702
01:39:28.374 --> 01:39:32.201
<c.p>to talk to the president about and I’m not going to have time.”

1703
01:39:32.201 --> 01:39:37.794
So it lulled just a minute when they was going to somebody else I said, “Mr. President, real quick.”

1704
01:39:37.794 --> 01:39:39.855
I said, “I got a quick question.”

1705
01:39:39.855 --> 01:39:45.154
I said, “I got a whole list of things here that I need to talk to you about.

1706
01:39:45.154 --> 01:39:48.981
Are we going to have an opportunity to visit with you and this?”

1707
01:39:48.981 --> 01:39:51.042
He looked at me like big brother.

1708
01:39:51.042 --> 01:39:55.752
He said, “Hal,” called me by my first name like he’d known me all my life.

1709
01:39:55.752 --> 01:39:57.224
Had his sleeves rolled up.

1710
01:39:57.224 --> 01:40:01.051
And he looked at me and said, “Hal,” he said, “You don’t worry.

1711
01:40:01.051 --> 01:40:04.584
We’re going to take all the time we need in this meeting.

1712
01:40:04.584 --> 01:40:09.000
We’ll make sure we get to every item that you have on that list.”

1713
01:40:09.000 --> 01:40:14.279
And then he comes back around the table and gets to me and looks over at me.

1714
01:40:14.279 --> 01:40:16.142
He’s like, “Hal what you got?”

1715
01:40:16.142 --> 01:40:18.626
I said, “Well we have this major infrastructure.

1716
01:40:18.626 --> 01:40:20.489
These major powerlines down right here.

1717
01:40:20.489 --> 01:40:25.147
We . . . this is . . . We can’t get the power grid going until we get these done.”

1718
01:40:25.147 --> 01:40:30.737
“General [Pratt?] I need you to get the Black Hawks down here and make sure we get those.”

1719
01:40:30.737 --> 01:40:32.289
Boy just started firing orders.

1720
01:40:32.289 --> 01:40:33.221
Everything I had.

1721
01:40:33.221 --> 01:40:33.532
Bam!

1722
01:40:33.532 --> 01:40:33.842
Bam!

1723
01:40:33.842 --> 01:40:34.153
Bam!

1724
01:40:34.153 --> 01:40:36.637
He was the president of the United States.

1725
01:40:36.637 --> 01:40:39.432
Most powerful man in the world right there sitting.

1726
01:40:39.432 --> 01:40:42.847
And it was just . . . and then the FEMA number expedited assistance.

1727
01:40:42.847 --> 01:40:48.437
Wanted to make sure that our people were going to get their two thousand dollars on expedited assistance.

1728
01:40:48.437 --> 01:40:54.337
He said, “Hal we’re going to try to get everybody that we can to get all the expedited assistance.

1729
01:40:54.337 --> 01:40:56.821
We got all our staff working on that.

1730
01:40:56.821 --> 01:40:59.616
We’re going to do that and get that done.”

1731
01:40:59.616 --> 01:41:06.758
He was sitting right next to Dave Paulison, “Dave I want to make sure . . . FEMA make sure southwest Louisiana gets all expedited assistance.

1732
01:41:06.758 --> 01:41:08.932
They get all the assistance they need.

1733
01:41:08.932 --> 01:41:12.658
We don’t want to get caught bottled down in bureaucracy and paperwork.”

1734
01:41:12.658 --> 01:41:16.074
It was just like, “Wow this guy is . . .” So we met.

1735
01:41:16.074 --> 01:41:21.663
And I remember when I got up from the table he walked over to me and grabbed me,

1736
01:41:21.663 --> 01:41:26.011
shook my hand, then he kind of . . . Then he grabbed me by the shoulder.

1737
01:41:26.011 --> 01:41:28.184
He said, “Hey, today’s the worst day.

1738
01:41:28.184 --> 01:41:30.358
It’s going to get better from here.”

1739
01:41:30.358 --> 01:41:31.289
And it did.

1740
01:41:31.289 --> 01:41:32.532
And when we went

1741
01:41:32.532 --> 01:41:37.811
<c.p>out on the tarmac and they had a . . . where you talk to the press and all that.

1742
01:41:37.811 --> 01:41:42.779
And he was so good, and knew what he was doing, and he was so genuine.

1743
01:41:42.779 --> 01:41:46.816
I won’t let people say a bad word about George Bush around me.

1744
01:41:46.816 --> 01:41:53.026
He helped southwest Louisiana recover as quick as it could and he gave us all the firepower that we needed.

1745
01:41:53.026 --> 01:41:57.063
And then we had really good leaders in southwest Louisiana that took charge.

1746
01:41:57.063 --> 01:42:01.721
Whether it was from Mark McMurry our parish administrator, the mayors of all the cities,

1747
01:42:01.721 --> 01:42:07.000
D. Gremillion of the OEP . . . Everybody was working hand in hand and it moved forward quickly.

1748
01:42:07.000 --> 01:42:10.144
And that’s why you don’t hear much about Rita.

1749
01:42:10.144 --> 01:42:12.939
And the other thing is, is the people.

1750
01:42:12.939 --> 01:42:15.035
It’s the people of southwest Louisiana.

1751
01:42:15.035 --> 01:42:17.131
Westlake, Mossville, every community through here.

1752
01:42:17.131 --> 01:42:20.973
We didn’t want a hand out, we wanted a hand up.

1753
01:42:20.973 --> 01:42:25.514
And once we got hat hand up, then that’s where we moved forward.

1754
01:42:25.514 --> 01:42:35.296
And that’s where the people . . . they said that a load of plywood would come to an area in New Orleans and people would stand there and look at,

1755
01:42:35.296 --> 01:42:37.741
“Well what do we do with that?”

1756
01:42:37.741 --> 01:42:46.823
A load of plywood come to southwest Louisiana the people would start unloading it on the trucks, taking it to people’s houses, and repairing their roofs.

1757
01:42:46.823 --> 01:42:55.207
The difference . . . The culture of the people of southwest Louisiana was one of the major reasons that you never heard much about Hurricane Rita.

1758
01:42:55.207 --> 01:43:01.845
We didn’t have . . . we had really good police protection with . . . I don’t remember who was working days or nights,

1759
01:43:01.845 --> 01:43:05.687
but we’d get reports from both . . . the sheriff had maybe days,

1760
01:43:05.687 --> 01:43:11.626
and the . . . Don Dixon the chief of police of Lake Charles, former FBI agent, had night shift.

1761
01:43:11.626 --> 01:43:14.071
And the entire parish was being secured.

1762
01:43:14.071 --> 01:43:28.743
They had planes flying above us with surveillance and stuff like . . . It was the safest parish in . . . or the safest place probably in the United States with all the surveillance that was over us because they had a daylight to dawn curfew.

1763
01:43:28.743 --> 01:43:33.633
And if you really kind of stepped out of your house they were watching.

1764
01:43:33.633 --> 01:43:37.476
I had one lady call me and say, “Hey we don’t

1765
01:43:37.476 --> 01:43:40.969
<c.p>have any police protection over her in the [Terrace?] area.”

1766
01:43:40.969 --> 01:43:43.065
I said, “Yes ma’am you do.”

1767
01:43:43.065 --> 01:43:52.846
I said, “You’re just not seeing them but they’re completely around you and the police would be there in a heartbeat if there was anybody outside moving around.”

1768
01:43:52.846 --> 01:44:00.881
In fact, there’s the story of the sheriff going outside and the helicopter putting the spotlight on him at one point in time.

1769
01:44:00.881 --> 01:44:05.422
But the people gave us time to kind of get everything back together.

1770
01:44:05.422 --> 01:44:15.553
Get the infrastructure needs and then the people started filtering back into the parish under the knowledge that they knew that we didn’t have fire departments and police departments.

1771
01:44:15.553 --> 01:44:21.142
So if you’re out there cutting limbs with a chainsaw and you gash your leg open,

1772
01:44:21.142 --> 01:44:24.985
there’s not going to be an ambulance come get you, okay?

1773
01:44:24.985 --> 01:44:31.971
There may not be much of an emergency room, a triage area, at the hospital to take care of you.

1774
01:44:31.971 --> 01:44:38.259
So if you come back into the parish . . . And southwest Louisiana is made up of some great people.

1775
01:44:38.259 --> 01:44:40.355
Some just . . . all walks of life.

1776
01:44:40.355 --> 01:44:42.451
Black, white, creole, good old boys.

1777
01:44:42.451 --> 01:44:45.595
And they want to take care of their stuff.

1778
01:44:45.595 --> 01:44:49.787
And they came back and they cut limbs off their neighbor’s houses,

1779
01:44:49.787 --> 01:44:57.472
they repaired what they needed to repair to keep any other damage from happening, emptying freezers, doing whatever they needed to do.

1780
01:44:57.472 --> 01:45:09.000
Yeah that was one of the positive things out of this negative is we didn’t have electricity and there was a lot of people that had a lot of shrimp and fried . . .

1781
01:45:09.000 --> 01:45:09.667
<v ARSENEAULT>Barbeque time.

1782
01:45:09.667 --> 01:45:10.333
<v MCMILLIN>Barbeque time.

1783
01:45:10.333 --> 01:45:17.000
Steaks . . . the first couple days we ate pretty good because that stuff was going to either spoil or be eaten.

1784
01:45:17.000 --> 01:45:21.667
So that night we had some pretty good cooks cooking some of that stuff.

1785
01:45:21.667 --> 01:45:25.333
Wow, what a surreal time to be a part of that.

1786
01:45:25.333 --> 01:45:30.333
One of the things that ended up happening, I didn’t have a place to stay.

1787
01:45:30.333 --> 01:45:31.667
My house was destroyed.

1788
01:45:31.667 --> 01:45:33.000
My wife was in

1789
01:45:33.000 --> 01:45:35.667
<c.p>Florida with her brother and stuff like this.

1790
01:45:35.667 --> 01:45:39.000
So I was kind of moving from place to place.

1791
01:45:39.000 --> 01:45:43.333
Well I ended up at the radio station, town square now radio station.

1792
01:45:43.333 --> 01:45:49.000
They had a couch up front, but they had air conditioning because they had a . . . generator going.

1793
01:45:49.000 --> 01:45:52.333
So they had air conditioning because man, it was hot.

1794
01:45:52.333 --> 01:45:57.667
And I got to sleep on that red couch up front for two or three days.

1795
01:45:57.667 --> 01:46:03.333
Well when I would do the full day of what I was doing as police juror president,

1796
01:46:03.333 --> 01:46:12.000
I would come in and sit on the radio and visit with some of the radio disc jockeys, and visit about what was going on.

1797
01:46:12.000 --> 01:46:16.821
Well what I didn’t know . . . that was before iPhones and internet . . . radio pup or whatever,

1798
01:46:16.821 --> 01:46:20.357
different apps you could have went on and listened to it.

1799
01:46:20.357 --> 01:46:25.179
But people were out in their trucks and cars listening to what was going on.

1800
01:46:25.179 --> 01:46:30.321
The information that I was giving updates on and what we were doing in the parish.

1801
01:46:30.321 --> 01:46:33.857
And although it was a crude mode of communication, it worked.

1802
01:46:33.857 --> 01:46:39.643
And people say, “Well Hal man I remember sitting out in the car,” when they’d come back and,

1803
01:46:39.643 --> 01:46:47.679
“We were sitting out in the car listening to you on the radio telling us all about what all was going on in the parish.

1804
01:46:47.679 --> 01:46:52.500
And what areas . . . and what was . . . from . . . I had a number of calls from buddies.

1805
01:46:52.500 --> 01:46:55.071
That’s when I first learned about texting, too.

1806
01:46:55.071 --> 01:46:57.643
Because they had Blackberrys and they were texting.

1807
01:46:57.643 --> 01:47:05.357
We didn’t have any communication because cell phones weren’t working back then very much, but when I could get a call from somebody like,

1808
01:47:05.357 --> 01:47:10.821
“Hey man would you ride by my house and check and see if my house is okay?”

1809
01:47:10.821 --> 01:47:20.786
Because they couldn’t come back in the parish because we had a curfew on the parish at that point in time and not letting people back in because it wasn’t safe.

1810
01:47:20.786 --> 01:47:22.714
Lot of interesting things went on.

1811
01:47:22.714 --> 01:47:24.000
Lot of . . . really . . .

1812
01:47:24.000 --> 01:47:26.448
<v ARSENEAULT>Any looting problems, or anything like that?

1813
01:47:26.448 --> 01:47:29.946
<v MCMILLIN>No, that’s what I was going to tell you earlier.

1814
01:47:29.946 --> 01:47:33.094
The sheriff and Don Dixon the chief of police,

1815
01:47:33.094 --> 01:47:41.139
I think they had a crew of people come in from the Houston area that were kind of going to do some looting.

1816
01:47:41.139 --> 01:47:46.386
And the surveillance nabbed them, caught them, probably arrested them and moved them out quickly.

1817
01:47:46.386 --> 01:47:53.381
So quickly got back to the people that were going to come here of a criminal aspect, don’t go there.

1818
01:47:53.381 --> 01:47:55.480
They’re on top of their game.

1819
01:47:55.480 --> 01:47:56.179
Great sheriff.

1820
01:47:56.179 --> 01:47:57.578
Great chief of police.

1821
01:47:57.578 --> 01:48:01.426
Don Dixon and Tony Mancuso and all their sheriff department folks.

1822
01:48:01.426 --> 01:48:02.475
We were safe.

1823
01:48:02.475 --> 01:48:04.574
We were safe in Calcasieu Parish.

1824
01:48:04.574 --> 01:48:07.022
You don’t hear any horror stories here.

1825
01:48:07.022 --> 01:48:13.318
I mean I don’t even think we had any deaths that were attributed to the hurricane at all.

1826
01:48:13.318 --> 01:48:16.117
And that’s because the people listened, they evacuated.

1827
01:48:16.117 --> 01:48:24.861
Now, if I would have stayed home in my house and hid in the bathroom during the storm, that oak tree would have killed me.

1828
01:48:24.861 --> 01:48:26.960
It came right through my bathroom.

1829
01:48:26.960 --> 01:48:31.507
It hit my house so hard it cracked the slab on my house.

1830
01:48:31.507 --> 01:48:36.753
And we tore that house completely down and built a brand new house right there.

1831
01:48:36.753 --> 01:48:37.803
Had good neighbors.

1832
01:48:37.803 --> 01:48:40.251
I didn’t want to leave my neighborhood.

1833
01:48:40.251 --> 01:48:40.601
Westlake.

1834
01:48:40.601 --> 01:48:42.000
I love it.

1835
01:48:42.000 --> 01:48:45.813
<v ARSENEAULT>I know FEMA got a lot of criticism in new Orleans.

1836
01:48:45.813 --> 01:48:47.546
Did you have kind of . . .?

1837
01:48:47.546 --> 01:48:49.972
<v MCMILLIN>No, no they did much better here.

1838
01:48:49.972 --> 01:48:50.319
Yeah.

1839
01:48:50.319 --> 01:48:52.398
They came to the table here.

1840
01:48:52.398 --> 01:48:55.171
They did much better here in Calcasieu Parish.

1841
01:48:55.171 --> 01:49:04.183
I mean it’s a system of bureaucracy that has to be done so people don’t abuse the system, but they paid out a lot of money.

1842
01:49:04.183 --> 01:49:10.076
They got the money out to the people and the claims that were . . . needed to be claimed.

1843
01:49:10.076 --> 01:49:12.502
They helped the people of Calcasieu Parish.

1844
01:49:12.502 --> 01:49:18.394
It was incidence . . . everybody . . . you’re not going to be a hundred percent . . . can’t go without making mistakes,

1845
01:49:18.394 --> 01:49:21.861
but they . . . FEMA did a good job in Calcasieu Parish.

1846
01:49:21.861 --> 01:49:22.207
I

1847
01:49:22.207 --> 01:49:26.713
<c.p>give them a much higher grade than what they did in New Orleans.

1848
01:49:26.713 --> 01:49:29.139
Of course I only hearsay New Orleans.

1849
01:49:29.139 --> 01:49:37.112
That was one of the things I used to say when we’d have a tough decision, “Well what they do in New Orleans?

1850
01:49:37.112 --> 01:49:38.498
Let’s don’t do that.”

1851
01:49:38.498 --> 01:49:39.884
Learn from their mistakes.

1852
01:49:39.884 --> 01:49:42.657
But that was . . . They were devastated down there.

1853
01:49:42.657 --> 01:49:44.044
We were devastated here.

1854
01:49:44.044 --> 01:49:49.590
I think if you look in the top ten list of disasters in the United States,

1855
01:49:49.590 --> 01:49:55.135
Rita still ranked very high up in the top ten, but you don’t hear about it.

1856
01:49:55.135 --> 01:49:59.641
And you just attribute it that yes, there was some good leadership here,

1857
01:49:59.641 --> 01:50:06.574
but it was the people . . . It was the people that really made the difference in southwest Louisiana of all cultures.

1858
01:50:06.574 --> 01:50:07.614
Of all races.

1859
01:50:07.614 --> 01:50:09.000
We were one.

1860
01:50:09.000 --> 01:50:11.352
<v ARSENEAULT>What makes southwest Louisiana so special?

1861
01:50:11.352 --> 01:50:17.624
<v MCMILLIN>It’s . . . a mentality of good fun, hardworking people that are God fearing . . . It’s a great place.

1862
01:50:17.624 --> 01:50:20.760
We don’t have a lot of negativity here.

1863
01:50:20.760 --> 01:50:23.896
We don’t have a lot of racial divide.

1864
01:50:23.896 --> 01:50:25.464
We all are one.

1865
01:50:25.464 --> 01:50:26.640
It’s a gumbo.

1866
01:50:26.640 --> 01:50:30.952
It’s a melting pot of people and everybody likes having fun.

1867
01:50:30.952 --> 01:50:32.128
Everybody likes smiling.

1868
01:50:32.128 --> 01:50:38.008
There’s a . . . Whether it’s showing off their best gumbo that they can cook, or jambalaya,

1869
01:50:38.008 --> 01:50:46.240
or having an adult beverage and smiling and laughing and listening to good Cajun music, Zydeco music, rock and roll music.

1870
01:50:46.240 --> 01:50:49.376
This is the greatest place in the world.

1871
01:50:49.376 --> 01:50:50.944
I love this place.

1872
01:50:50.944 --> 01:50:52.512
I love southwest Louisiana.

1873
01:50:52.512 --> 01:50:54.864
Its . . . I’m blessed to be here.

1874
01:50:54.864 --> 01:51:00.352
I enjoy seeing the rest of the world, but I always love coming home.

1875
01:51:00.352 --> 01:51:08.976
And every time I come across the I-10 Bridge and I look across whether I’m going toward Westlake and see the industries,

1876
01:51:08.976 --> 01:51:12.896
which I always tell my kids, “Look that’s our livelihood.”

1877
01:51:12.896 --> 01:51:15.640
And at night it looks like Oz.

1878
01:51:15.640 --> 01:51:16.424
It’s neat.

1879
01:51:16.424 --> 01:51:25.832
And if you knew what all was going on out there it’s really neat because there’s a lot of lot of really smart things

1880
01:51:25.832 --> 01:51:33.280
<c.p>happening in that plant to make the chemicals and the products they do to make our life much easier.

1881
01:51:33.280 --> 01:51:40.728
Or if you’re coming over into Lake Charles and you’re looking out and you’re looking out into the swamp,

1882
01:51:40.728 --> 01:51:47.000
or even looking into the lake in the skyline of Lake Charles . . . I love it.

1883
01:51:47.000 --> 01:51:48.860
And then where we’re at too,

1884
01:51:48.860 --> 01:51:54.440
I mean thirty miles to the north . . . and this is where I get back into doing my accents,

1885
01:51:54.440 --> 01:51:57.230
but I say this, thirty miles to the north,

1886
01:51:57.230 --> 01:52:04.980
“Hey man you can get up there to DeQuincy and them old boys love to be in them piney woods, they hunt deer with dogs,

1887
01:52:04.980 --> 01:52:12.420
and they drink a cold beer, and just ride around their pickup trucks in the dirt roads up there and have a good time.”

1888
01:52:12.420 --> 01:52:15.830
And thirty miles to the south, “[. . .?] How y’all are? [Speaking French].”

1889
01:52:15.830 --> 01:52:17.380
They duck hunt down there . . .

1890
01:52:17.380 --> 01:52:18.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Cajun.

1891
01:52:18.000 --> 01:52:23.504
<v MCMILLIN>They got the Cajun culture, and the jambalaya and the gumbo, and you know what?

1892
01:52:23.504 --> 01:52:27.906
They drinking the same beer up there as they are down there.

1893
01:52:27.906 --> 01:52:29.374
So they’re very hospitable.

1894
01:52:29.374 --> 01:52:30.842
It’s a melting pot.

1895
01:52:30.842 --> 01:52:33.777
And I-10 cuts through the middle of us.

1896
01:52:33.777 --> 01:52:36.712
And I’m not making fun of those voices.

1897
01:52:36.712 --> 01:52:38.180
Those are my friends.

1898
01:52:38.180 --> 01:52:46.252
And I can talk like the guys from DeQuincy and I can talk like the guys from Cameron Parish, which . . . Great people.

1899
01:52:46.252 --> 01:52:49.921
Great people is what makes a difference in this area.

1900
01:52:49.921 --> 01:52:53.223
<v ARSENEAULT>Well that’s about all the questions that I had.

1901
01:52:53.223 --> 01:52:59.827
I wanted to close with is there anything you wanted to add about your time serving your ward,

1902
01:52:59.827 --> 01:53:04.230
or your childhood, or your family, or all of your extracurricular activities?

1903
01:53:04.230 --> 01:53:05.698
<v MCMILLIN>Its . . . life’s been good.

1904
01:53:05.698 --> 01:53:14.137
I’ve been blessed with good health and I often say that although I’m sixty, I’ve had enough fun for a ninety year old.

1905
01:53:14.137 --> 01:53:17.806
I have fun pretty much every day of my life.

1906
01:53:17.806 --> 01:53:27.345
I try to not be negative although sometimes you have bad days no matter what you do, but nobody’s the boss of my shoes but me.

1907
01:53:27.345 --> 01:53:30.647
And nobody’s the boss of my attitude but me.

1908
01:53:30.647 --> 01:53:36.885
And if you could instill that into more people, that if you’re not happy change your attitude.

1909
01:53:36.885 --> 01:53:37.986
You control it.

1910
01:53:37.986 --> 01:53:42.388
If you’re not happy in those shoes, change to some other shoes.

1911
01:53:42.388 --> 01:53:46.058
Whatever it takes to make you happy and have fun.

1912
01:53:46.058 --> 01:53:47.892
And be good to people.

1913
01:53:47.892 --> 01:53:49.727
Live life to the fullest.

1914
01:53:49.727 --> 01:53:54.129
We’re a small dash between 1955 dash and whatever day I die,

1915
01:53:54.129 --> 01:54:00.000
but I’m trying to live every day to the fullest and have fun doing that.

1916
01:54:00.000 --> 01:54:07.030
And seems like the more you take a positive attitude in life, the more positive things will happen to you.

1917
01:54:07.030 --> 01:54:08.787
Don’t dwell on the negatives.

1918
01:54:08.787 --> 01:54:12.653
There’s going to be rain showers to make those flowers grow.

1919
01:54:12.653 --> 01:54:14.411
Don’t dwell on the negatives.

1920
01:54:14.411 --> 01:54:20.035
I hate that my dad died in my arms, I hate that I lost my mom,

1921
01:54:20.035 --> 01:54:24.252
but I had them and I’ve grown from each of those situations.

1922
01:54:24.252 --> 01:54:30.579
And just when something happens negative, try to grow from that situation to make yourself a better person.

1923
01:54:30.579 --> 01:54:33.743
I try to be a better Hal every day.

1924
01:54:33.743 --> 01:54:37.960
I’m not the same guy that threw tomatoes at Ms. Trahan’s car.

1925
01:54:37.960 --> 01:54:39.718
I’ve grown through that stage.

1926
01:54:39.718 --> 01:54:46.748
I’m not the same guy that may have argued a call or something on a baseball field many years ago.

1927
01:54:46.748 --> 01:54:52.020
You got to grow every day and you got to make yourself a better person.

1928
01:54:52.020 --> 01:54:54.480
And nobody’s responsible for that, but you.

1929
01:54:54.480 --> 01:55:00.104
So it all falls back to hey, be God fearing, love the fellow man around you,

1930
01:55:00.104 --> 01:55:04.673
try to obey the good Lord’s big ten and hope you get in.

1931
01:55:04.673 --> 01:55:05.376
<v ARSENEAULT>Very good.

1932
01:55:05.376 --> 01:55:11.000
And since this is a Mossville project, how would you like Mossville to be remembered?

1933
01:55:11.000 --> 01:55:12.604
<v MCMILLIN>Mossville is a great community.

1934
01:55:12.604 --> 01:55:14.209
Mossville is a hometown community.

1935
01:55:14.209 --> 01:55:17.096
It’s a bedroom community right next to the industries.

1936
01:55:17.096 --> 01:55:19.342
It had a lot of great people.

1937
01:55:19.342 --> 01:55:27.364
And I think really what you need to . . . not the material things of Mossville need to be . . . so much remembered as the people of Mossville.

1938
01:55:27.364 --> 01:55:29.289
Don’t let the people be forgotten.

1939
01:55:29.289 --> 01:55:33.139
There’s a lot of great folks that have came out of Mossville.

1940
01:55:33.139 --> 01:55:36.668
I could start trying to name them, but I’d miss them.

1941
01:55:36.668 --> 01:55:39.235
Richard Lee, we talked about earlier, great guy.

1942
01:55:39.235 --> 01:55:40.519
Loved the rabbit hunt.

1943
01:55:40.519 --> 01:55:42.444
Him and his brother Kenny Lee.

1944
01:55:42.444 --> 01:55:43.406
Kenny’s still around.

1945
01:55:43.406 --> 01:55:45.011
He loves to rabbit hunt.

1946
01:55:45.011 --> 01:55:50.465
You got a lot of great families in Mossville that have a lot of really good heritage.

1947
01:55:50.465 --> 01:55:53.674
Coach Williams . . . and we got a road named after him.

1948
01:55:53.674 --> 01:55:56.561
Coach Williams, a mentor to many a young man.

1949
01:55:56.561 --> 01:56:02.979
He was a coach at our high school during some pretty rough times, but he never saw black and white.

1950
01:56:02.979 --> 01:56:03.941
He saw hearts.

1951
01:56:03.941 --> 01:56:09.075
So if you look at people that way, and Mossville’s got a lot of great people.

1952
01:56:09.075 --> 01:56:11.000
Remember the people of Mossville.

1953
01:56:11.000 --> 01:56:18.806
And yes, from the growth of his area and their homes being encompassed in some of that growth we’re going to miss that.

1954
01:56:18.806 --> 01:56:28.648
But we’re not going to miss our kids and grandkids who are going to be able to grow up here in Southwest Louisiana with really good jobs and opportunities.

1955
01:56:28.648 --> 01:56:30.685
And not just jobs at Sasol.

1956
01:56:30.685 --> 01:56:35.097
I personally work in a job that is an offshoot of the industries.

1957
01:56:35.097 --> 01:56:39.170
And for every one industrial job, they say there’s seven offshoot jobs.

1958
01:56:39.170 --> 01:56:42.224
So they don’t have to work in the plant.

1959
01:56:42.224 --> 01:56:49.012
They can work at a restaurant that feeds people in the plant, they can work as a business development guy,

1960
01:56:49.012 --> 01:56:54.442
they can work as a car dealership selling cars to people that work in the plant.

1961
01:56:54.442 --> 01:56:58.855
There’s so many opportunities because we have good paying jobs in southwest Louisiana.

1962
01:56:58.855 --> 01:57:03.606
I was talking to a senator yesterday from Monroe and he said, “My gosh.

1963
01:57:03.606 --> 01:57:04.285
Southwest Louisiana.

1964
01:57:04.285 --> 01:57:09.715
You guys must pray real hard down there because you’re so blessed with so many opportunities.”

1965
01:57:09.715 --> 01:57:14.127
Now, there is some growing pains in these opportunities, but please don’t forget

1966
01:57:14.127 --> 01:57:22.273
<c.p>the people of Westlake, and Mossville, and all southwest Louisiana that have laid the path for us to be better stewards of this community.

1967
01:57:22.273 --> 01:57:29.400
And to the people that are coming, try to do the best you can to keep it going forward so thirty,

1968
01:57:29.400 --> 01:57:36.188
forty years from now people will look at your generation and say, “Hey those people did a lot for us.”

1969
01:57:36.188 --> 01:57:36.867
Ship channels.

1970
01:57:36.867 --> 01:57:37.885
A basic idea.

1971
01:57:37.885 --> 01:57:40.939
Who would have thought Lake Charles needed ship channels?

1972
01:57:40.939 --> 01:57:45.352
They thought of that many years ago and it’s been a tremendous opportunity.

1973
01:57:45.352 --> 01:57:56.891
What can I do now as an elected official here in my era that will live an impact of my community and change the quality of life and make it better for southwest Louisiana?

1974
01:57:56.891 --> 01:57:58.927
That’s what I’m trying to do.

1975
01:57:58.927 --> 01:57:59.606
Thank y’all.

1976
01:57:59.606 --> 01:58:00.624
God bless y’all.

1977
01:58:00.624 --> 01:58:03.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Alright I think we got everything.

1978
01:58:03.000 --> 01:58:03.200
<c.p>[End Tape 4641.

1979
01:58:03.000 --> 01:58:03.200
End Session I.]
